(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch05.json"]={"n": 5, "title": "The Roman Empire from Constantinople (330–1204)", "events": [{"id": "H3-001", "date": "330", "date_text": "11 May 330", "precision": "day", "title": "Constantinople dedicated: Pontus is ruled from the Bosporus", "summary": "Constantine dedicates his new capital on the Bosporus. For the next 874 years the Pontic coast is governed from a city that lies at the far end of its own sea route: Constantinople, Sinope, Amisos, Trapezous. That sea lane, not the mountain roads, ties the Greeks of Pontus to the centre of the empire throughout this chapter.", "people": ["Constantine I"], "sources_primary": ["Chronicon Paschale, s.a. 330"], "sources_modern": ["Dagron 1974, Naissance d'une capitale", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 18 (the sea \"has always been\" the main means of communication along the Pontic coast; Constantinople to Erzurum overland about 25 days, by sea to Trebizond and over the mountains about a third of that)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "imperial capital"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "waypoint 1 of the coastal sea route"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.302, "lon": 36.335, "role": "waypoint 2 of the coastal sea route"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 3, eastern end of the sea route"}]}, {"id": "H3-002", "date": "386", "date_text": "386 (tradition); first firm documents 14th c.", "precision": "year", "title": "The Soumela tradition: Barnabas and Sophronios", "summary": "Monastic tradition holds that two monks of Athens, Barnabas (born Basil) and his nephew Sophronios, guided by a vision of the Virgin, brought an icon ascribed to St Luke to a cave on Mt Mela above the Pyxites valley and founded Soumela in 386, under Theodosius I; both are said to have died in 412. A farmer-monk Christopher is said to have restored the house after a 7th-century sack. No contemporary text, inscription or building of the 4th–7th centuries supports this; the monastery's documented history begins with the Grand Komnenoi, above all the chrysobull of Alexios III (December 1364). The earliest surviving written forms of the legend are 18th-century: a history and office by Dionysios, archbishop of Chaldia (c. 1760, printed at Bucharest 1769), and the office with a 'sacred history' composed by Neophytos Kausokalyvites from older manuscripts sent to him from Soumela and since lost (Leipzig 1775) (Kyriakidis 1898). Chrysanthos (1933, p. 470) repeats the tradition that the church was consecrated 'in 386' by the bishop of Trapezous, and has the house refounded by Christophoros of Gozara.", "people": ["Barnabas of Soumela", "Sophronios of Soumela", "Christopher of Soumela", "Theodosius I"], "sources_primary": ["Office (akolouthia) and 'sacred history' of Barnabas, Sophronios and Christopher (feast 18 August), composed by Neophytos Kausokalyvites at the request of Parthenios Metaxopoulos, first printed Leipzig 1775; synopsis and office by Dionysios of Chaldia (c. 1760; printed Bucharest 1769): Kyriakidis 1898, pp. ια΄–ιβ΄, ις΄", "Chrysobull of Alexios III Megas Komnenos for Soumela, 1364/5 (preserved in later copies)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 254 (Soumela \"supposedly founded\" by Barnabas and Sophronios; \"difficult to separate tradition from fact\"; \"the tradition, and hence the existence of Soumela, seems to have been established by the tenth century\"; patronage of John II, Alexios II and Basil; rebuilt by Alexios III in 1360-65)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος", "Bryer 1980, The Empire of Trebizond and the Pontos (Variorum)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 468–476; p. 470", "Bryer 1979, 'The late Byzantine monastery in town and countryside' (repr. Bryer 1980, VI), p. 229 (Alexios III ‘refounded Soumela’ in 1364)", "Eastmond ed. 2016, Byzantium’s Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 175 (Asp-Talwar: Alexios III re-founded Soumela in 1364)", "Kyriakidis 1898, Ἱστορία τῆς... μονῆς... Σουμελᾶ, introduction pp. ια΄–ιβ΄, ις΄"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The date 386 is tradition only. Bryer & Winfield (1985) treat the foundation story as tradition, but hold that 'the tradition, and hence the existence of Soumela, seems to have been established by the tenth century', without giving a source for that date. The first firm evidence is of the 13th–14th centuries, above all the chrysobull of Alexios III of December 1364, which Bryer uses without reservation (he doubts the authenticity of the chrysobulls for Vazelon, 1386, and Choutoura, 1365). Earlier, lost versions of the legend were named by Metaxopoulos (Akakios the Sabaite, whom he placed about 680, Nikodemos of Phasis, and an anonymous author), but Kyriakidis knew nothing of them beyond that statement.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Soumela monastery (Mt Mela)", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.658, "role": "traditional foundation site"}]}, {"id": "H3-003", "date": "387", "date_end": "421", "date_text": "C. 387 (partition); c. 415–421 (Theodosiopolis founded)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Armenia partitioned; Theodosiopolis founded as the Roman bulwark", "summary": "Rome and Persia divided the kingdom of Armenia c. 387; Rome received the smaller western part (Armenia Interior), which brought the frontier to the upper Euphrates and the Araxes, three to four days south and east of Trapezous. Under Theodosius II the fortress-city of Theodosiopolis (Karin, modern Erzurum) was built as the Roman anchor of the new frontier. From now on the Pontic hinterland is a military rear for the Persian front.", "people": ["Theodosius I", "Shapur III", "Theodosius II", "Anatolius (magister militum)"], "sources_primary": ["Procopius, Buildings 3.1.8–15 and 3.5.2–12", "Movses Khorenatsi 3.42 ('the division of Armenia into two, under the two Arsacid kings', in the service of the Persians and the Greeks) and 3.59 (the general Anatolius builds Karin and names it Theodosiopolis) (Classical Armenian text with glosses: 12historians.armeniancathedral.org, book t06Khor3_42 and t06Khor3_59)", "Lazar Parpetsi 1.6"], "sources_modern": ["Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II", "Garsoïan, in Hovannisian ed. 1997, The Armenian People from Ancient to Modern Times, vol. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The partition is dated between 384 and 390, most often about 387. Procopius credits Theodosius II with founding Theodosiopolis, Armenian tradition the general Anatolius; the dates range from about 415 to 421.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Theodosiopolis (Karin, Erzurum)", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "new frontier fortress"}, {"name": "Satala", "lat": 40.026, "lon": 39.596, "role": "older legionary base behind the frontier"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "port supplying the frontier"}]}, {"id": "H3-004", "date": "400", "date_text": "C. 386–c. 400 (state shown in the Notitia Dignitatum)", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Pontic provinces c. 400: Helenopontus, Pontus Polemoniacus, Armenia I and II", "summary": "In the late Roman diocese of Pontica the old kingdom of Pontus was split into small provinces. Helenopontus (earlier Diospontus, renamed after Constantine's mother Helena) had its metropolis at Amaseia and included Amisos, Sinope, Zela, Ibora and Andrapa. Pontus Polemoniacus had its metropolis at Neocaesarea and included Komana, Polemonion, Kerasous and Trapezous. Inland, Armenia Minor was divided under Theodosius I (c. 386) into Armenia I (Sebasteia, Nikopolis, Satala, Koloneia, Sebastopolis) and Armenia II (Melitene).", "people": ["Theodosius I"], "sources_primary": ["Notitia Dignitatum, Oriens 1 and 25", "Laterculus Veronensis (c. 314: Diospontus, Pontus Polemoniacus, Armenia Minor)", "Hierocles, Synekdemos 701–703 (state c. 527)"], "sources_modern": ["Jones 1964, The Later Roman Empire", "Talbert ed. 2000, Barrington Atlas of the Greek and Roman World (Pontus sheets)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The renaming of Diospontus as Helenopontus (under Constantine, before the 330s) and the division of Armenia Minor (usually about 386) are not exactly dated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "metropolis of Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "metropolis of Pontus Polemoniacus"}, {"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "metropolis of Armenia I"}, {"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "metropolis of Armenia II"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "city of Pontus Polemoniacus"}, {"name": "Kerasous", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.389, "role": "city of Pontus Polemoniacus"}, {"name": "Polemonion", "lat": 41.04, "lon": 37.52, "role": "city of Pontus Polemoniacus (approximate)"}, {"name": "Komana Pontike", "lat": 40.357, "lon": 36.625, "role": "city of Pontus Polemoniacus"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.302, "lon": 36.335, "role": "city of Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "city of Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.302, "lon": 35.886, "role": "city of Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Andrapa (Neoclaudiopolis)", "lat": 41.143, "lon": 35.455, "role": "city of Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Ibora", "lat": 40.66, "lon": 36.57, "role": "city of Helenopontus (approximate; identified with İverönü)"}, {"name": "Nikopolis", "lat": 40.193, "lon": 38.14, "role": "city of Armenia I (approximate)"}, {"name": "Satala", "lat": 40.026, "lon": 39.596, "role": "legionary fortress, Armenia I"}, {"name": "Koloneia", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "city of Armenia I (modern Şebinkarahisar)"}]}, {"id": "H3-005", "date": "407", "date_text": "14 September 407", "precision": "day", "title": "John Chrysostom dies at Komana on the road to Pityous", "summary": "The deposed bishop of Constantinople, exiled first to Kokousos in Armenia II (404), was being marched to Pityous at the far eastern end of the Black Sea when he died at Komana in Pontus, at the shrine of the martyr Basiliskos. The route shows how the Pontic road system linked the capital with the Caucasian coast, and that Pityous, not Trapezous, marked the empire's edge on the sea.", "people": ["John Chrysostom", "Arcadius"], "sources_primary": ["Palladius, Dialogus de vita Ioannis Chrysostomi 11", "Socrates, Historia ecclesiastica 6.21", "Sozomen, Historia ecclesiastica 8.28"], "sources_modern": ["Kelly 1995, Golden Mouth", "Bryer & Winfield 1978, 'Cities of Heraclius', BMGS 4 (repr. Bryer 1980, I), p. 28 (Chrysostom’s exile to Pityous, 407)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 168 (in 407 Pityous was chosen as St John Chrysostom's place of exile, \"who died on the way there\")"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 1 (departure, 404)"}, {"name": "Caesarea in Cappadocia", "lat": 38.722, "lon": 35.487, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Kokousos", "lat": 38.03, "lon": 36.5, "role": "waypoint 3, first place of exile (approximate)"}, {"name": "Komana Pontike", "lat": 40.357, "lon": 36.625, "role": "waypoint 4, place of death"}, {"name": "Pityous", "lat": 43.16, "lon": 40.34, "role": "intended place of exile, never reached"}]}, {"id": "C2-005", "date": "451", "date_text": "451", "precision": "year", "title": "Atarbios of Trapezous at Chalcedon", "summary": "At the Council of Chalcedon Atarbios, 'bishop of the city of Trapezous' ('Ἀτάρβιος ἐπίσκοπος τῆς πόλεως Τραπεζοῦντος'), signed also for his metropolitan Dorotheos of Neocaesarea, whose deacon Photeinos sat as his representative. Trapezous was then a suffragan see of Neocaesarea in Pontus Polemoniacus.", "people": ["Atarbios of Trapezous", "Dorotheos of Neocaesarea"], "sources_primary": ["Acts of the Council of Chalcedon (ACO II)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 188–189"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chalcedon (Kadıköy)", "lat": 40.99, "lon": 29.03, "role": "council"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "see"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.59, "lon": 36.95, "role": "metropolis"}]}, {"id": "C2-006", "date": "518", "date_text": "518", "precision": "year", "title": "Antipatros of Trapezous at the synod of Constantinople", "summary": "At the 'resident synod' of Constantinople in 518 against the Monophysites, bishop Antipatros of Trapezous signed the report against Severus of Antioch submitted to Patriarch John ('Ἀντίπατρος ἐπίσκοπος Τραπεζοῦντος ὑπέγραψα'). Chrysanthos takes him to be the next bishop after Atarbios.", "people": ["Antipatros of Trapezous", "John II of Constantinople", "Severus of Antioch"], "sources_primary": ["Acts of the synod of Constantinople 518 (in the acts of 536, ACO III)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 189"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "synod"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "see"}]}, {"id": "H3-006", "date": "522", "date_text": "522", "precision": "year", "title": "Lazica turns to Rome: King Tzath I baptised in Constantinople", "summary": "Tzath I, king of the Lazi in old Colchis, broke with Persia, came to Constantinople, was baptised and crowned with Justin I as godfather. Persia saw the loss of its client as a casus belli, and Lazica's control of the passes and the eastern Black Sea coast became a cause of the wars of 526–562. For Pontus it meant that the Roman sphere now ran unbroken along the coast from Trapezous to the Caucasus.", "people": ["Tzath I", "Justin I", "Kavad I"], "sources_primary": ["Malalas 17.9", "Chronicon Paschale, s.a. 522", "Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6015", "Procopius, Wars 2.15.2"], "sources_modern": ["Braund 1994, Georgia in Antiquity", "Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "baptism and coronation"}, {"name": "Archaeopolis (Nokalakevi)", "lat": 42.371, "lon": 42.195, "role": "chief fortress of the Lazic kings"}, {"name": "Phasis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "Roman port at the mouth of the Phasis"}]}, {"id": "H3-007", "date": "528", "date_text": "C. 528", "precision": "year", "title": "The Tzanoi subdued and baptised; an army command for Armenia", "summary": "The Tzanoi, highlanders of the Pontic Alps between Trapezous and Armenia who lived by raiding the coast and the Armenian plains, were defeated by Sittas and persuaded to accept Christianity and serve in the army. Justinian cut roads through their forests, built forts (Horonon, Barchon, Sisilisson, Bourgousnoes, Schamalinichon, Tzanzakon) and a church at Schamalinichon. In the same years he created a separate field command for Armenia (magister militum per Armeniam), with Sittas as its first holder.", "people": ["Sittas", "Justinian I"], "sources_primary": ["Procopius, Wars 1.15.19–25", "Procopius, Buildings 3.6.1–26", "Malalas 18.10", "Codex Justinianus 1.29.5"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1966–67, 'Some notes on the Laz and Tzan', Bedi Kartlisa 21–23", "Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II", "Mitford 2018, East of Asia Minor", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 35, 37 (Horonon proposed as the successor of Salmalasso, near Gelengeç/Lori, at the meeting of the roads of Romania, Persarmenia and Tzanika); pp. 50, 304, 311 (Zindanlar Arazi at the Kanis-Leri confluence tentatively Longini Fossatum/Bourgousnoes); p. 312 (Tzanzakon taken to be Tzanicha, Canca); pp. 313, 315 (Schamalinichon assigned to the Leri district but \"cannot be located\")"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "None of Procopius' forts among the Tzanoi is securely located. Bryer & Winfield (1985) offer tentative sites: Horonon near Gelengeç (Lori), on the borders of Erzurum, Gümüşhane and Erzincan; Bourgousnoes at Zindanlar Arazi, Murathanoğulları; Tzanzakon at Tzanicha (Canca); and Schamalinichon somewhere in the Leri valley. None is secured by an inscription. The Tzanoi are often equated with Strabo's Sannoi and with the later 'Chan', a Georgian name for the Laz; the continuity is debated (Bryer).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tzanica (land of the Tzanoi)", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 40.1, "role": "highland region between Trapezous, Rhizaion and Paipert (approximate centre; the forts are unlocated)"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "coastal city raided by the Tzanoi"}, {"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "seat of the new Armenian command"}]}, {"id": "C2-007", "date": "535", "date_end": "536", "date_text": "June 535 – before 13 March 536 (patriarchate); condemned May–June 536", "precision": "month", "title": "Anthimos of Trapezous becomes patriarch and is deposed", "summary": "Anthimos, bishop of Trapezous (probably a Syrian, in Chrysanthos's view), took part in the conference of 532 with the anti-Chalcedonians, became patriarch of Constantinople in June 535, was deposed before 13 March 536 under pressure from Pope Agapetus and condemned in May–June 536. The church of Trapezous was left 'widowed and without a husband' ('χήραν καὶ ἄνανδρον').", "people": ["Anthimos of Trapezous", "Justinian I", "Theodora", "Pope Agapetus"], "sources_primary": ["Acts of the synod of Constantinople 536 (ACO III)", "Justinian, Novel 42"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 190–200"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "patriarchate"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "first see"}]}, {"id": "H3-008", "date": "536", "date_text": "Novel 28 (16 July 535) and Novel 31 (18 March 536)", "precision": "day", "title": "Justinian redraws the map: Trapezous goes to Armenia I", "summary": "Justinian abolished Pontus Polemoniacus. Neocaesarea and Polemonion were joined to Helenopontus under a moderator at Amaseia, while Trapezous and Kerasous were placed in a new Armenia I (Magna) with Theodosiopolis, Satala, Nikopolis and Koloneia, under a proconsul at Justinianopolis (Bazanis or Leontopolis). The old Armenia I around Sebasteia became Armenia II, with Sebastopolis, Brisa, Komana (taken from Pontus Polemoniacus) and Zela (taken from Helenopontus); the old Armenia II around Melitene became Armenia III, and the southern satrapies Armenia IV. The coast from Trapezous east was thus administered with the Armenian frontier, not with the rest of Pontus.", "people": ["Justinian I"], "sources_primary": ["Justinian, Novel 28 (De moderatore Helenoponti)", "Justinian, Novel 31 (De quattuor Armeniis)", "Procopius, Buildings 3.1.16–29"], "sources_modern": ["Adontz 1970, Armenia in the Period of Justinian (tr. Garsoïan)", "Jones 1964, The Later Roman Empire", "Hewsen 2001, Armenia: A Historical Atlas", "Harutyunyan, H. Zh. 2023, 'The question of Byzantine Armenia in Ancient Armenian Geography (Ashkharatsuyts)', Science Journal of Volgograd State University, History 28/6, pp. 194–196 (quotes Novel 31 in Russian translation: Armenia I with Theodosiopolis, Satala, Nikopolis, Koloneia, Trapezous and Kerasous, capital Justinianopolis; Armenia II with Sebasteia, Sebastopolis, Komana 'from the former Pontus Polemoniacus', Zela 'from Helenopontus' and Brisa; Armenia III with Arka, Arabissos, Ariaratheia, the other Komana (Chryse) and Kokousos)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Trapezous was in Armenia I but was not its capital: the provincial capital was Justinianopolis (Bazanis or Leontopolis), whose site is uncertain, though some modern summaries call Trapezous the capital. Novel 31, as quoted by Harutyunyan (2023), puts Komana and Zela in the new Armenia II; Harutyunyan identifies Justinianopolis with Tzoumina.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "seat of the moderator of Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Justinianopolis (Bazanis)", "lat": 39.78, "lon": 40.39, "role": "metropolis of Armenia I (location uncertain; placed near Tercan)"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "transferred to Armenia I"}, {"name": "Kerasous", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.389, "role": "transferred to Armenia I"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "transferred to Helenopontus"}, {"name": "Polemonion", "lat": 41.04, "lon": 37.52, "role": "transferred to Helenopontus (approximate)"}, {"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "metropolis of the new Armenia II"}, {"name": "Komana Pontike", "lat": 40.357, "lon": 36.625, "role": "assigned to Armenia II"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.302, "lon": 35.886, "role": "assigned to Armenia II"}, {"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "metropolis of Armenia III"}, {"name": "Martyropolis", "lat": 38.137, "lon": 41.003, "role": "metropolis of Armenia IV"}, {"name": "Satala", "lat": 40.026, "lon": 39.596, "role": "city of Armenia I"}, {"name": "Nikopolis", "lat": 40.193, "lon": 38.14, "role": "city of Armenia I (approximate)"}, {"name": "Koloneia", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "city of Armenia I"}, {"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "city of Armenia I"}]}, {"id": "H3-009", "date": "540", "date_text": "Under Justinian (527–565), works undated", "precision": "decade", "title": "Justinian's works at Trapezous and on the coast", "summary": "Procopius records that Justinian gave Trapezous, which lacked water, an aqueduct called after the martyr Eugenios, and restored most of the churches of Trapezous and Amaseia. He also built 'a novel system of defences' at Rhizaion and new walls at Satala, Koloneia and Theodosiopolis. Procopius says nothing about walls at Trapezous. The gate inscription reported by Miller, once taken as evidence for Justinianic walls, records a renewal of the city's buildings: so Eastmond (2016, p. 11), following Bryer & Winfield (1985, p. 182). Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 216-217) reads the inscription: Justinian 'renewed with munificence the public buildings of the city' ('ἀνενέωσε ἐν φιλοτιμίᾳ τὰ δημόσια κτίσματα τῆς πόλεως') through the zeal of the most God-loving Eirenaios, dated indiction 5, year 480 (ΥΠ) of the Trapezuntine era, i.e. 542; he makes Eirenaios bishop. The text speaks of public buildings, not walls.", "people": ["Justinian I"], "sources_primary": ["Procopius, Buildings 3.7.1–3 (Trapezous, Amaseia, Rhizaion)", "Procopius, Buildings 3.4.1–7 (Satala, Koloneia)", "Procopius, Buildings 3.5.2–12 (Theodosiopolis)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 182 (Procopius: aqueduct of the martyr Eugenios, churches restored; the Tabakhane gate inscription, CIG 8636, now lost: Justinian completed the restoration of the buildings of Trebizond through Bishop Eirenaios in indiction 5, year 480 of the local era from 63/64, i.e. AD 542)", "Miller 1926, Trebizond, p. 11 (the gate inscription)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 216–217", "Eastmond ed. 2016, Byzantium’s Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 11 (after Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 182): Justinian ‘set up an inscription recording his “renewal” of its buildings’", "Bryer & Winfield 1978, 'Cities of Heraclius', BMGS 4 (repr. Bryer 1980, I), p. 27 (Justinian refortified Rhizaion rather than Sourmaina)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 331 (Procopius on Justinian's \"novel system of defenses\" at Rhizaion)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 146 (Procopius: Justinian strengthened Koloneia; its earlier blocked castle gateway perhaps of his time)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The aqueduct is secure (Procopius). Walls built by Justinian at Trapezous are not attested: the one inscription, recorded by Tournefort in 1701 and since lost (CIG 8636), speaks of renewed public buildings. Bryer & Winfield (1985) and Chrysanthos read the bishop's name as Eirenaios and the date as indiction 5, year 480 of the city's era, that is AD 542.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "aqueduct of St Eugenios; churches restored"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "churches restored"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 40.522, "role": "new defences"}, {"name": "Satala", "lat": 40.026, "lon": 39.596, "role": "new circuit wall"}, {"name": "Koloneia", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "restored"}, {"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "ditch, walls and towers rebuilt"}]}, {"id": "H3-010", "date": "541", "date_text": "541", "precision": "year", "title": "Petra betrayed to Chosroes: the Lazic War begins", "summary": "The Lazi resented the Roman garrison and the trade monopoly that the general John Tzibus ran from his new fortress of Petra (built c. 535). King Gubazes II invited Chosroes I, who marched into Lazica and took Petra; John Tzibus was killed. Persia now held a port on the Black Sea about 150 km east of Trapezous.", "people": ["Chosroes I", "Gubazes II", "John Tzibus"], "sources_primary": ["Procopius, Wars 2.15.1–35", "Procopius, Wars 2.17.1–28"], "sources_modern": ["Braund 1994, Georgia in Antiquity", "Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Petra (Tsikhisdziri)", "lat": 41.767, "lon": 41.754, "role": "Roman fortress taken by the Persians"}, {"name": "Archaeopolis (Nokalakevi)", "lat": 42.371, "lon": 42.195, "role": "Lazic royal fortress"}]}, {"id": "H3-011", "date": "549", "date_text": "548–549", "precision": "year", "title": "Persian aims at the Black Sea; the first Roman siege of Petra", "summary": "Procopius says that Chosroes meant to remove the Lazi and settle Persians in their land, gaining a base from which a fleet could reach Constantinople and the Huns of the steppe; he sent ship timber to Lazica, which was destroyed by lightning. Gubazes changed sides again in 548, and Dagisthaios besieged Petra with 7,000 Romans and 1,000 Tzanoi but failed to take it before Mermeroes relieved it.", "people": ["Chosroes I", "Gubazes II", "Dagisthaios", "Mermeroes"], "sources_primary": ["Procopius, Wars 2.28.17–30, esp. 2.28.23 (Chosroes' plan)", "Procopius, Wars 2.29.1–3 (the timber)", "Procopius, Wars 2.29.10 (7,000 Romans and 1,000 Tzanoi) to 2.30.48 (siege of Petra)"], "sources_modern": ["Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II", "Braund 1994, Georgia in Antiquity"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Whether Chosroes really planned a fleet on the Black Sea, or Procopius gave voice to Roman fears, is debated; most modern scholars take the threat as real but exaggerated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Petra (Tsikhisdziri)", "lat": 41.767, "lon": 41.754, "role": "besieged by the Romans"}, {"name": "Phasis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "Roman base"}]}, {"id": "H3-012", "date": "551", "date_text": "550–551", "precision": "year", "title": "Petra stormed and razed; Archaeopolis holds", "summary": "After a Persian defeat on the river Hippis (550), the Roman general Bessas stormed Petra in spring 551 and razed it so that it could not be used again. Mermeroes then attacked Archaeopolis, the chief Lazic fortress, and was beaten off, but occupied Kotais, Uthimereos and the lands of eastern Lazica.", "people": ["Bessas", "Mermeroes", "Gubazes II"], "sources_primary": ["Procopius, Wars 8.8 (Hippis)", "Procopius, Wars 8.11–12 (Petra)", "Procopius, Wars 8.13–14 (Archaeopolis)", "Procopius, Buildings 3.7.7"], "sources_modern": ["Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II", "Braund 1994, Georgia in Antiquity"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Petra (Tsikhisdziri)", "lat": 41.767, "lon": 41.754, "role": "stormed and razed by Bessas"}, {"name": "Archaeopolis (Nokalakevi)", "lat": 42.371, "lon": 42.195, "role": "Persian assault repulsed"}, {"name": "Kotais (Kutaisi)", "lat": 42.271, "lon": 42.705, "role": "occupied by the Persians"}, {"name": "River Hippis (Tskhenistsqali)", "lat": 42.25, "lon": 42.45, "role": "Persian defeat, 550 (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H3-013", "date": "554", "date_end": "555", "date_text": "554–555", "precision": "year", "title": "Defeat at Telephis and the murder of King Gubazes", "summary": "Mermeroes routed a larger Roman–Lazic army at Telephis by a feigned retreat (554). The Roman commanders Martin and Rusticus, quarrelling with Gubazes, had him murdered (555); the Lazi nearly went over to Persia, and Justinian put the murderers on trial (Agathias reports the trial at length). Tzath II was installed as the new king.", "people": ["Mermeroes", "Gubazes II", "Martin", "Rusticus", "John (brother of Rusticus)", "Tzath II"], "sources_primary": ["Agathias, Histories 2.19–22", "Agathias, Histories 3.2–4 and 4.1–11"], "sources_modern": ["Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II", "Braund 1994, Georgia in Antiquity"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Telephis", "lat": 42.23, "lon": 42.6, "role": "Roman defeat (location approximate)"}, {"name": "Phasis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "Roman headquarters in Lazica"}]}, {"id": "H3-014", "date": "556", "date_text": "556", "precision": "year", "title": "Persian siege of Phasis fails", "summary": "The Persian commander Nachoragan attacked the Roman port of Phasis and was routed, which ended Persian hopes of taking the Lazic coast. A truce followed in 557.", "people": ["Nachoragan", "Martin"], "sources_primary": ["Agathias, Histories 3.19–28"], "sources_modern": ["Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Phasis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "besieged; Persian defeat"}]}, {"id": "H3-015", "date": "557", "date_end": "558", "date_text": "C. 557–558", "precision": "year", "title": "The Tzanoi rise again", "summary": "The Tzanoi, Christian and enrolled in the army for a generation, rose and plundered the roads around Trapezous and Rhizaion. Theodore, himself a Tzan by birth, defeated them, and a tribute was imposed. The episode shows that the highlands behind Trapezous were still only loosely held.", "people": ["Theodore the Tzan"], "sources_primary": ["Agathias, Histories 5.1–2"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1966–67, 'Some notes on the Laz and Tzan'"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Dated 557 or 558.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tzanica", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 40.1, "role": "highland homeland of the Tzanoi (approximate)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 40.522, "role": "coastal district raided"}]}, {"id": "H3-016", "date": "562", "date_text": "Late 561 or early 562", "precision": "year", "title": "The Fifty Years' Peace: Lazica stays Roman", "summary": "Peter the Patrician and the Persian envoy Yazdgushnasp agreed a peace near Dara. Persia withdrew from Lazica; Rome paid 30,000 nomismata (about 400 lb of gold) a year, with the first seven years in advance. The whole eastern Black Sea coast was now Roman or Roman-client, so the Pontus faced no sea-borne threat from the east.", "people": ["Justinian I", "Chosroes I", "Peter the Patrician", "Yazdgushnasp"], "sources_primary": ["Menander Protector, fr. 6.1 (Blockley)"], "sources_modern": ["Blockley 1985, The History of Menander the Guardsman", "Greatrex & Lieu 2002, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars, Part II"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Date: late 561 or early 562.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Dara", "lat": 37.178, "lon": 40.944, "role": "where the treaty was concluded"}, {"name": "Archaeopolis (Nokalakevi)", "lat": 42.371, "lon": 42.195, "role": "Lazica, evacuated by Persia"}]}, {"id": "H3-017", "date": "611", "date_end": "615", "date_text": "611–615", "precision": "year", "title": "The Persian war reaches the edge of Pontus", "summary": "In the war of 602–628 the Persians took Theodosiopolis (c. 610), occupied Caesarea in Cappadocia (611–612), and burned Euchaita in the Armeniakon (615). The Pontic coast itself is not reported as attacked, but its hinterland was exposed for the first time since the 3rd century.", "people": ["Shahin", "Priscus", "Heraclius"], "sources_primary": ["Sebeos, History ch. 34", "Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6103", "Miracles of St Theodore of Euchaita (for 615)"], "sources_modern": ["Kaegi 2003, Heraclius", "Trombley 1985, 'The decline of the seventh-century town: the exception of Euchaita'", "Haldon ed. 2018, The Archaeology of Medieval Avkat"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "surrendered to the Persians, c. 610"}, {"name": "Caesarea in Cappadocia", "lat": 38.722, "lon": 35.487, "role": "occupied, 611–612"}, {"name": "Euchaita (Avkat)", "lat": 40.57, "lon": 35.22, "role": "burned, 615 (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H3-018", "date": "622", "date_end": "623", "date_text": "April 622 – winter 622/3", "precision": "month", "title": "Herakleios' first campaign: the army winters in Pontus", "summary": "Herakleios left Constantinople on 4 April 622, sailed to Pylai, drilled his army through the summer, and in autumn marched into Cappadocia, where he defeated Shahrbaraz. He went back to the capital and left the army to winter 'in Pontus'. No source names the winter quarters; Trapezous is not mentioned.", "people": ["Heraclius", "Shahrbaraz"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6113", "George of Pisidia, Expeditio Persica"], "sources_modern": ["Kaegi 2003, Heraclius, pp. 112–116", "Howard-Johnston 1999, 'Heraclius' Persian campaigns', War in History 6, pp. 1–44"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The sites of the battle of 622 and of the winter quarters are unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Pylai", "lat": 40.66, "lon": 29.27, "role": "waypoint 2, landing and training camp (approximate)"}, {"name": "Cappadocia (Caesarea area)", "lat": 38.722, "lon": 35.487, "role": "waypoint 3, autumn victory (exact site unknown)"}, {"name": "Pontus (Amaseia region)", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "waypoint 4, winter quarters 622/3 (exact place unknown)"}]}, {"id": "H3-019", "date": "624", "date_end": "628", "date_text": "624–628", "precision": "year", "title": "Herakleios' great campaigns: Armenia, Lazica and the Turkish alliance", "summary": "In 624 Herakleios marched east by Caesarea and the upper Euphrates (by Satala and Theodosiopolis, according to some reconstructions) to Dvin and Ganzak. After the battle on the Saros (625), Theophanes has him winter at Sebasteia. In 626 he moved to Lazica, and in 627 he joined the Turks (the 'Khazars' of the Greek sources) before Tiflis, then struck south to Nineveh (12 December 627). Trapezous was the natural port for the Lazic phase, but no primary source names it; the claim that the army wintered at Trebizond in 625/6 (Norwich, often repeated) conflicts with Theophanes. Bryer and Winfield (1978) argue that he wintered in 625 in the valleys around Bathys Rhyax, west of Sebasteia, and that in 626 he used the old Roman camp at Sourmaina, renamed Herakleia (Araklı, 27 km east of Trapezous), as his base for contact with Constantinople and the Khazars and as his port for Lazica; they suggest the name commemorates the birth there of his son Heraklonas.", "people": ["Heraclius", "Chosroes II", "Shahrbaraz", "Ziebel (Tong Yabghu?)"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6114–6118", "Sebeos, History ch. 38–39", "Movses Daskhurantsi, History of the Albanians, book 2", "George of Pisidia, Heraclias", "Theophanes, Chronographia, ed. de Boor, p. 314 (winter 625)", "Sebeos, trans. Macler, pp. 62, 80–84", "Movses Dasxuranci, trans. Dowsett, pp. 78–87", "Eutychios of Alexandria, Annales (Arabic), names Trebizond", "Georgian chronicle (interpolated passage, Van Esbroeck p. 93), names Trebizond"], "sources_modern": ["Kaegi 2003, Heraclius, pp. 120–130", "Howard-Johnston 1999, War in History 6", "Zuckerman 2002, 'Heraclius in 625', REB 60", "Bryer & Winfield 1978, 'Cities of Heraclius', BMGS 4 (repr. Bryer 1980, I), pp. 16–30, esp. 17 n. 7 (sources), 18 (Theophanes, de Boor p. 314: through Sebasteia, wintering beyond the Halys in 625), 21 (Bathys Rhyax), 23–25 (Trebizond in the sources), 26–30 (Sourmaina/Herakleia)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 327, 329 (Heraclius probably used Sourmaina as a supply base in 622-623 and in 626 to receive the Khazars, send help to Constantinople and sail to Lazica; the Roman camp of Sousourmaina at Canayer on Araklı Burunu perhaps reused or rebuilt in 626, when it took the name Herakleia); p. 10 (the camp of Hyssos which became Herakleia (Araklı), \"where we suggest that Heraclius was stationed in 625\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The routes are reconstructions, argued over by Howard-Johnston, Zuckerman and Kaegi. On the Pontic port of 626: Vasiliev (1935, Russian-American), followed by Stratos (1968, Greek) and Baynes (1913, British), names Trebizond, after an interpolated Georgian chronicle and Eutychios of Alexandria (died 940), the only sources that name it; Bryer & Winfield (1978, British) propose Herakleia-Sourmaina (Araklı), from the stations of the Notitia, the route up the Hyssos to Satala and the place-name Herakleia, and call it a proposal, not proof.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 1 (25 March 624)"}, {"name": "Caesarea in Cappadocia", "lat": 38.722, "lon": 35.487, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Satala", "lat": 40.026, "lon": 39.596, "role": "waypoint 3 (reconstructed)"}, {"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "waypoint 4 (reconstructed)"}, {"name": "Dvin", "lat": 40.004, "lon": 44.577, "role": "waypoint 5, sacked 624"}, {"name": "Ganzak", "lat": 36.98, "lon": 46.1, "role": "waypoint 6 (approximate)"}, {"name": "River Saros", "lat": 37.0, "lon": 35.32, "role": "waypoint 7, battle 625"}, {"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "waypoint 8, winter 625/6 (Theophanes)"}, {"name": "Phasis (Lazica)", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "waypoint 9, Lazica 626–627"}, {"name": "Tiflis", "lat": 41.693, "lon": 44.801, "role": "waypoint 10, joint siege with the Turks, 627"}, {"name": "Nineveh", "lat": 36.36, "lon": 43.15, "role": "waypoint 11, victory 12 December 627"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "probable supply port for Lazica (not attested)"}, {"name": "Herakleia (Araklı), approx.", "lat": 40.939, "lon": 40.058, "role": "proposed base of Herakleios in 626 (Bryer & Winfield 1978)"}]}, {"id": "H3-020", "date": "630", "date_text": "C. 620s–640s", "precision": "decade", "title": "Anania of Shirak studies mathematics at Trapezous", "summary": "The Armenian scholar Anania of Shirak, after failing to find a teacher in Armenia, went to Trapezous and studied for eight years with Tychikos, a teacher who had learned in Constantinople, Alexandria and Rome and drew pupils from the capital. Anania's autobiography is the best evidence for Trapezous as a centre of Greek learning in the 7th century, and for Armenians coming to the Pontic coast to study. He found Tychikos ‘at the shrine of Saint Eugenia’ (an error for Eugenios), which makes the passage one of the earliest witnesses to the martyr’s shrine at Trapezous (Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 65–66).", "people": ["Anania of Shirak", "Tychikos"], "sources_primary": ["Anania Shirakatsi, Autobiography (tr. Conybeare 1897, Byzantinische Zeitschrift 6)"], "sources_modern": ["Greenwood 2011, 'A reassessment of the life and mathematical problems of Anania Širakacʿi', Revue des études arméniennes 33", "Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 65–66 (Anania found Tychikos ‘at the shrine of Saint Eugenia’, an error for Eugenios)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 168 and n. 21 (Ananias of Shirak mentions the martyr at Trebizond in the early 7th century; \"for Eugenia one should presumably read Eugenios\"); p. 223 (Tychikos)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The dates of Anania's stay are uncertain, between the 620s and the 640s; Rosenqvist (1996) puts it 'apparently c. AD 600', without arguing the point.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "school of Tychikos"}]}, {"id": "H3-021", "date": "640", "date_end": "663", "date_text": "640 and 663/4", "precision": "year", "title": "Arab raids reach Euchaita", "summary": "The first Arab raids into the Armeniakon hit Euchaita, the shrine town of St Theodore: an attack under Mu'awiya c. 640, then a capture in 663/4 when the raiders plundered the town, burned the saint's church and wintered there. Over the next two centuries the Arab raids struck the inland towns of Pontus (Euchaita, Amaseia, the plains of Dazimon) but are not recorded as crossing the Pontic Alps to Trapezous.", "people": ["Mu'awiya"], "sources_primary": ["Miracles of St Theodore of Euchaita", "Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6156: 'Abd al-Rahman b. Khalid invades Roman territory and winters there (Euchaita not named); Elias of Nisibis: wintering in AH 44 = 663/4 (both as summarised by PBE, 'Abd ar-Rahman 9', pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D00/F16.htm)"], "sources_modern": ["Trombley 1985, in Vryonis ed., Byzantine Studies in Honor of Milton V. Anastos, pp. 65–90", "Haldon ed. 2018, The Archaeology of Medieval Avkat"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The dates (640 or 644; 663 or 664) are argued from hagiography and chronicles, and Trombley's reading is the standard one. The Prosopography of the Byzantine Empire dates the winter raids of 'Abd al-Rahman b. Khalid to 663/4–666/7 from Theophanes, Tabari and Elias of Nisibis; Theophanes does not name Euchaita, so the town's capture rests on the Miracles of St Theodore.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Euchaita (Avkat)", "lat": 40.57, "lon": 35.22, "role": "raided 640, taken 663/4 (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H3-022", "date": "655", "date_end": "684", "date_text": "C. 655–684", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Paulicians begin at Kibossos (Kibossa) near Koloneia", "summary": "According to Peter of Sicily, Constantine (called Silvanus), born at Mananalis near Samosata of Armenia under Constans II, founded the dualist Paulician community at the fort of Kibossos near Koloneia and lived there for twenty-seven years. An imperial official, Symeon, sent by an emperor whom Peter does not name (probably Constantine IV or Justinian II), had him stoned by his own adopted son Justus; three years later Symeon returned secretly to Kibossos and led the community himself as 'Titus'. The Pontic interior thus saw the birth of the sect that later built a state at Tephrike and fought the emperors.", "people": ["Constantine-Silvanus", "Symeon-Titus", "Constantine IV"], "sources_primary": ["Peter of Sicily, Historia (ed. D. Papachryssanthou, in Astruc et al. 1970, Travaux et Mémoires 4, pp. 3–67), §§ 94–105 (Constantine at Kibossos; the church of 'Macedonia'), § 101 (the name Silvanus), §§ 103–111 (Symeon/Titus; Justus), as summarised by PBE, 'Konstantinos 1' and 'Symeon 1' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D43/F12.htm, D72/F68.htm)", "Photios, Narratio de Manichaeis", "Peter of Sicily, Historia 41 (PG 104, col. 1276)"], "sources_modern": ["Lemerle 1973, 'L'histoire des Pauliciens d'Asie Mineure', Travaux et Mémoires 5", "Garsoïan 1967, The Paulician Heresy", "Bryer 1977, 'Excursus on Mananalis, Samosata of Armenia and Paulician geography' (repr. Bryer 1980, II), p. 83 (Peter of Sicily, Histoire 41 = PG 104, col. 1276: Constantine born at Mananalis; Mananalis a district c. 110 km NNE of Samosata of Armenia)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 146-147 (Constantine-Sylvanos founded the Church of \"Macedonia\" at the castle of Kibossa near Koloneia in the 670s; condemned ca. 683 and killed by Justus south of Koloneia; Symeon-Titos killed before 687; Kibossa \"must be sought in the mountains round Koloneia\"; Sisorta Kale, 33 km WNW of Şebinkarahisar, a candidate on geographical likelihood only)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The chronology rests on hostile Greek sources; Garsoïan sees the Paulicians' roots in Armenian adoptionism, while Lemerle reads the Greek sources more literally. Kibossa has not been located, and the map shows Koloneia. Bryer & Winfield (1985) date the foundation at Kibossa to the 670s and Constantine-Silvanus' death to about 683, later than the usual start about 655, and suggest Sisorta Kale, 33 km west-north-west of Şebinkarahisar, for Kibossa, 'but there are no grounds, other than those of geographical likelihood'. Bryer (1977) places Mananalis, the founder's home district, in the southern Tuzlasuyu plain around Tercan.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kibossa", "lat": 40.29, "lon": 38.42, "role": "first Paulician community (location unknown; placed at Koloneia)"}, {"name": "Koloneia", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "nearest city"}, {"name": "Mananalis (southern Tuzlasuyu plain, near Tercan), approx.", "lat": 39.833, "lon": 40.383, "role": "birthplace of Constantine-Silvanus (Bryer 1977; mapped at Tercan, whose coordinates Bryer gives)"}]}, {"id": "H3-023", "date": "667", "date_end": "668", "date_text": "667/668 (Theophanes AM 6159; Chronicle to 1234: 26th year of Constans); 665/666 in Michael the Syrian", "precision": "year", "title": "Saborios' revolt: first clear record of the Armeniakon theme", "summary": "Saborios (Shapur), strategos of the Armeniakoi, rebelled against Constans II and offered, through his envoy Sergios, to subject the empire to the caliph Mu'awiya in return for help; an Arab force under Fadala was sent, and Constans's son Constantine sent an army under Nikephoros against him. Saborios died in a riding accident at a place called Adrianoupolis before the revolt spread. This is the first unambiguous literary record of the Armeniakon, the army corps settled in the old Pontus and Armenia Minor, with its headquarters at Amaseia.", "people": ["Saborios", "Constantine IV", "Nikephoros (patrikios)"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6159", "Chronicle to 1234, § 138; Michael the Syrian II 451–454 (977 Sel. = 665/666); Bar Hebraeus, Chronography pp. 100–101 (all as cited by PBE)"], "sources_modern": ["ODB 1991, 'Armeniakoi', p. 177", "Haldon 1997, Byzantium in the Seventh Century, pp. 214–216", "PBE, 'Saborios 1' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D67/F16.htm)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "A 'tourmarches of the Armeniakoi' appears in 629, so the corps may be older; whether it was already a territorial theme in the 660s is debated. Where Saborios died is disputed.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "headquarters of the Armeniakon"}, {"name": "Hadrianopolis in Bithynia", "lat": 40.94, "lon": 32.54, "certainty": "disputed", "role": "where Saborios died: 'Adrianoupolis', located near Melitene by PBE and in Phrygia Paroreios by Ramsay (Historical Geography p. 142); this point (Hadrianopolis in Honorias) is one candidate only"}]}, {"id": "C2-009", "date": "680", "date_text": "680–681", "precision": "year", "title": "Theodoros of Trapezous at the Sixth Ecumenical Council", "summary": "Theodoros, 'bishop of the city of the Trapezuntines' ('ἐπίσκοπος τῆς Τραπεζουντίων πόλεως'), signed the acts of the Sixth Ecumenical Council at Constantinople.", "people": ["Theodoros of Trapezous"], "sources_primary": ["Acts of the Sixth Ecumenical Council (Mansi XI)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 200–201"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "council"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "see"}]}, {"id": "H3-024", "date": "712", "date_text": "712", "precision": "year", "title": "Maslama takes Amaseia", "summary": "The Umayyad prince Maslama raided the Armeniakon and took Amaseia, its headquarters, and other forts. It is the deepest recorded Arab success in Pontus proper; the city was soon back in Roman hands.", "people": ["Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6204"], "sources_modern": ["Lilie 1976, Die byzantinische Reaktion auf die Ausbreitung der Araber", "Treadgold 1997, A History of the Byzantine State and Society"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Theophanes gives no detail on how long Amaseia was held.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "taken by the Arabs"}]}, {"id": "C2-010", "date": "787", "date_text": "787", "precision": "year", "title": "Christophoros 'of Phasis, that is of Trapezous', at the Second Council of Nicaea", "summary": "At Nicaea II, Christophoros signed as 'unworthy bishop of Phasis, that is of Trapezous' ('Χριστοφόρος ἀνάξιος ἐπίσκοπος τοῦ Φάσιδος, ἤτοι Τραπεζοῦντος'). From this Chrysanthos argues that Trapezous had already taken over the metropolitan rank of Phasis by 787, earlier than the mid-9th-century date (raising by Patriarch Methodios) given by Papadopoulos-Kerameus. Later lists call it 'metropolis of Trapezous of the eparchy of Lazike'.", "people": ["Christophoros of Trapezous"], "sources_primary": ["Acts of the Second Council of Nicaea (Mansi XIII)"], "sources_modern": ["Papadopoulos-Kerameus (mid-9th-century date)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 153–154; p. 201; list p. 789"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Trapezous became a metropolis either by 787 (Chrysanthos, from this signature) or about 843–847 (the Synaxarion of Athanasios; Papadopoulos-Kerameus).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicaea (İznik)", "lat": 40.429, "lon": 29.721, "role": "council"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "metropolis"}, {"name": "Phasis (Poti)", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "old metropolis of Lazike"}]}, {"id": "H3-025", "date": "790", "date_text": "C. 789–791", "precision": "decade", "title": "Armenians found Hamamashen (Hemşin)", "summary": "The Armenian historian Ghewond records that the prince Shapuh Amatuni and his son Hamam, fleeing Arab rule, crossed into Roman territory with about 12,000 people and were received by the emperor. Armenian tradition says Hamam rebuilt the ruined town of Tambur in the hills east of Rhizaion and called it Hamamashen, the origin of the Hemşin district and its Armenian-speaking people.", "people": ["Shapuh Amatuni", "Hamam Amatuni", "Constantine VI"], "sources_primary": ["Ghewond, History ch. 42 (tr. Arzoumanian 1982)"], "sources_modern": ["Simonian ed. 2007, The Hemshin (esp. Hachikian, pp. 146–147)", "Vaux 2001, Hemshinli"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figure of 12,000 is Ghewond's. The link between his migrants and Hamamashen comes from later Armenian tradition (Vardan, 13th century), and the date varies between 789 and 791.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Hamamashen (Hemşin)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 40.9, "role": "Armenian settlement in the hills above Rhizaion"}, {"name": "Rhizaion", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 40.522, "role": "nearest coastal town"}]}, {"id": "H3-026", "date": "811", "date_text": "February 811 (Theophanes) or 1 March 811", "precision": "month", "title": "Arabs seize the Armeniakon's pay near Euchaita", "summary": "In early 811, while Leo, strategos of the Armeniakoi (the future emperor Leo V), and a large force were escorting the pay of his troops, Arab raiders attacked them near Euchaita and captured all the money, thirteen hundred pounds of gold, and many of his men. Nikephoros I had Leo beaten and exiled for negligence. The loss shows how exposed the interior of Pontus still was in the early 9th century.", "people": ["Leo, strategos of the Armeniakon"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes, Chronographia AM 6303", "Theophanes Continuatus I 4 (Bonn p. 11): Leo beaten and exiled", "Scriptor Incertus 336 (Leo also tonsured)"], "sources_modern": ["Treadgold 1988, The Byzantine Revival 780–842", "PBE, 'Leon 15' (Leo V) (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D48/F30.htm)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Theophanes dates it to February 811; the Prosopography of the Byzantine Empire to 1 March 811, the first Saturday of Lent.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Euchaita (Avkat)", "lat": 40.57, "lon": 35.22, "role": "where the pay escort was attacked (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H3-027", "date": "819", "date_end": "842", "date_text": "C. 819–842 (theme certainly by 842/3)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Chaldia becomes a theme, with Trapezous as its capital", "summary": "Chaldia began as a tourma (division) of the Armeniakon, became a semi-separate doukaton or archontate, and was a full theme by c. 840 (Taktikon Uspensky, 842/3), perhaps from c. 819–824. Its strategos sat at Trapezous. In the 10th century he drew 20 lb of gold a year, half from the treasury and half from local revenue, mainly the kommerkion (customs) on the trade through Trapezous. Muslim writers give it 6 forts (Ibn Khurradadhbih) and 4,000 troops (Qudama); Ibn al-Faqih's 10,000 is exaggerated. Keltzene was detached in the early 10th century to the new theme of Mesopotamia. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 51-52) describes the core of Chaldia as three banda, of Trapezous, Gemora and Matzouka ('τὸ βάνδον Τραπεζοῦντος, τὸ βάνδον Γημωρᾶς καὶ τὸ βάνδον Ματσουκάων'), matching the Ottoman districts of Trabzon, Yomra and Maçka. Bryer (1975, p. 117) counts seven valley banda round Trebizond, west to east: Philabonites (Harşit), Trikomia (Akçaabat), Trebizond, Matzouka (Maçka), Gemora (Yomra), Sourmaina (Sürmene) and Rhizaion (Rize); all but the Philabonites kept their military structure until 1461.", "sources_primary": ["Taktikon Uspensky (ed. Oikonomides 1972)", "Constantine VII, De thematibus (ed. Pertusi 1952), with commentary pp. 138–139", "Constantine VII, De ceremoniis 2.50 (salaries)", "Ibn Khurradadhbih, Kitab al-masalik (BGA 6)", "Qudama ibn Ja'far, Kitab al-kharaj (BGA 6)", "Constantine Porphyrogennetos, De cerimoniis 2.50 (Reiske p. 697)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic exception', DOP 29, pp. 116 ff.", "Treadgold 1995, Byzantium and Its Army, p. 31", "ODB 1991, 'Chaldia'", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 51–52", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), p. 199 (De cerimoniis, Reiske p. 697 [= 2.50]: the strategos of Chaldia had a smaller salary because he took the kommerkion; only the strategos of Mesopotamia had a like arrangement)", "Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic exception', DOP 29 (repr. Bryer 1980, V), pp. 116–117 (the thema of Chaldia ‘from the 820’s’; the seven valleys round Trebizond as banda)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 182, 301, 316 n. 1 (Trebizond capital of the Chaldian theme ca. 824; a \"ducatus\" by the letter of Michael II of 10 April 824; a strategos of Trebizond in the Life of George of Amastris; taktikon Uspenskij 842/43; half the strategos' salary from the kommerkion, De cerimoniis, Bonn, 696-97; Ibn al-Faqih's 10,000 men)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Full theme status is dated about 819, 824 or 840. Chrysanthos describes the core of Chaldia as three banda, Bryer (1975) as probably seven; both rest on evidence of the 13th–15th centuries, so the division may be later than the theme.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "seat of the strategos/doux of Chaldia"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "inland fortress of Chaldia"}, {"name": "Keltzene (Erzincan plain)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.49, "role": "district detached to Mesopotamia, early 10th c."}, {"name": "Rhizaion", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 40.522, "role": "eastern coastal town of Chaldia"}]}, {"id": "H3-028", "date": "838", "date_text": "22 July 838", "precision": "day", "title": "Defeat at Anzen near Dazimon", "summary": "The emperor Theophilos was defeated by the Abbasid general Afshin at Anzen, a hill in the plain of Dazimon between Amaseia and Tokat. The Roman army broke on a rumour of the emperor's death, and the Abbasids went on to sack Ankyra and Amorion. It is the largest battle fought on Pontic soil in this chapter.", "people": ["Theophilos", "Afshin", "Umar al-Aqta", "Theophobos", "Manuel the Armenian"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus III 31–32 (Bonn pp. 126–128)", "Genesios III 13–14", "Chronicle to 1234, §§ 221–222; Bar Hebraeus p. 136 (as cited by PBE)", "al-Tabari, History (vol. 33, tr. Bosworth)"], "sources_modern": ["Haldon 2001, The Byzantine Wars, pp. 78–83", "Treadgold 1988, The Byzantine Revival 780–842", "Vasiliev 1935, Byzance et les Arabes I, p. 141", "Bryer & Winfield 1978, 'Cities of Heraclius', BMGS 4 (repr. Bryer 1980, I), p. 20 (Dazimon, ‘a fertile plain’, the modern Kaz Ovası on the Iris west of Dokeia/Tokat; an aplekton)", "PBE, 'Afshin 1' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D00/F76.htm)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 7-8 (Dazimon in the Dazimonites plain, Kaz Ovası, with Zela and Komana)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The army sizes are modern estimates (Romans 25,000–40,000, Abbasids 20,000–30,000). The year 838 is secure; the day, 22 July, rests on Vasiliev and Treadgold. Bar Hebraeus reports a loss of 3,000 men for Afshin in an encounter with Theophilos.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Dazimon (Dazmana) / Anzen", "lat": 40.4, "lon": 36.35, "role": "battlefield (approximate)"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "nearby theme capital"}]}, {"id": "H3-029", "date": "838", "date_end": "839", "date_text": "838–839", "precision": "year", "title": "The Khurramite Persians proclaim Theophobos emperor at Sinope", "summary": "Theophilos had settled Khurramite Persian refugees in the themes (fourteen thousand soldiers, in the Symeon Logothete chronicles) under a Persian commander, Theophobos. The Persians carried him off to Sinope and proclaimed him emperor against his will. They were then moved to Amastris and dispersed through the themes in regiments of two thousand, and Theophobos submitted, on promises of safety, to a fleet sent under Ooryphas. A reminder that 'Romans' in 9th-century Pontus included settled foreigners.", "people": ["Theophobos", "Theophilos"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus III 29 (Bonn pp. 124–125) and III 38 (pp. 135–136)", "Genesios III 5 and III 8", "Leo Grammaticus 215; Georgius Monachus Continuatus 793; Pseudo-Symeon 625–626 (the 14,000 Persians)"], "sources_modern": ["ODB 1991, 'Theophobos', pp. 2067–2068", "Treadgold 1988, The Byzantine Revival", "PBE, 'Theophobos 1' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D80/F66.htm)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 70-71 (in 834 Nasir, \"called Theophobos\", was proclaimed king at Sinope by a \"Persian\" garrison of 7,000 to 30,000 men; he allied himself with Theophilos and took refuge with him in Constantinople in 838; Theophanes Continuatus, Bonn ed., 112, 124-25)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The number of the Khurramites (14,000 to 30,000 in the sources) and Theophobos' fate are disputed; he was later held in the Boukoleon palace and killed in 842. Bryer & Winfield (1985) date the proclamation at Sinope to 834, with Theophobos taking refuge in Constantinople in 838.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "where Theophobos was proclaimed"}, {"name": "Amastris", "lat": 41.747, "lon": 32.386, "role": "second Persian base"}]}, {"id": "H3-030", "date": "842", "date_end": "863", "date_text": "C. 842 (doukaton); by 863 (theme)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Koloneia becomes a theme", "summary": "Koloneia, on the upper Lykos, was split off from the Armeniakon, first under a doux (Theophilos appointed Kallistos, c. 842) and by 863 under a full strategos. Its 10th-century extent ran from Koloneia to Neocaesarea, Nikopolis and Tephrike, with sixteen forts. Kallistos is remembered as having been handed over by the Paulicians of Koloneia to their co-religionists under Arab rule (Karbeas's following) and executed with the 42 Martyrs of Amorion on 6 March 845.", "people": ["Theophilos", "Kallistos"], "sources_primary": ["Constantine VII, De thematibus (ed. Pertusi 1952), pp. 141–142 commentary", "Acts of the 42 Martyrs of Amorion, BHG 1209–1214, ed. Vasilievskij & Nikitin 1905 (Mémoires de l'Académie impériale de St-Pétersbourg, 8e sér., 7.2): BHG 1213 (Michael Synkellos), pp. 22–36 ('Καλλίστου δουκός'); BHG 1209, p. 50 (among the first executed) (as cited by PBE, 'Kallistos 2')"], "sources_modern": ["ODB 1991, 'Koloneia' (Foss), p. 1138", "Oikonomides 1972, Les listes de préséance, p. 349", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 146-147 (a kleisoura first; Kallistos appointed doux of Koloneia in Theophilos' will, Theophilos d. 842; died with the Forty-two Martyrs on 6 March 845; a strategos in 863; the theme from east of Neokaisareia to Nikopolis and Tephrike, with sixteen strongholds)", "PBE, 'Kallistos 2' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D42/F12.htm)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether Koloneia was a frontier district (kleisoura) before it became a doukaton is debated (Oikonomides, reading al-Mas'udi).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Koloneia (Şebinkarahisar)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "capital of the theme"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "western limit"}, {"name": "Nikopolis", "lat": 40.193, "lon": 38.14, "role": "town of the theme (approximate)"}, {"name": "Tephrike (Divriği)", "lat": 39.371, "lon": 38.114, "role": "added after 878"}]}, {"id": "H3-031", "date": "843", "date_end": "878", "date_text": "843–878", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Paulician state at Tephrike", "summary": "After the persecution under the regent Theodora (843), the Paulician officer Karbeas fled to the emir of Melitene and built a fortified Paulician principality at Tephrike, on the southern edge of the theme of Koloneia. With Arab allies the Paulicians raided deep into Asia Minor; under Chrysocheir they reached Ankyra and Nicaea and sacked Ephesos (869/70).", "people": ["Karbeas", "Chrysocheir", "Theodora (regent)", "Umar al-Aqta"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus IV 16 (Bonn pp. 165–167) and IV 23 (pp. 176–177)", "Peter of Sicily, Historia 184–186"], "sources_modern": ["Lemerle 1973, Travaux et Mémoires 5", "Garsoïan 1967, The Paulician Heresy", "PBE, 'Karbeas 1' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D42/F45.htm)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figure of 100,000 Paulicians killed in 843 (Theophanes Continuatus) is not credible as a count. According to the Prosopography of the Byzantine Empire, Karbeas, an officer of the strategos of the Anatolikoi, deserted with 5,000 Paulicians after his father was killed in the persecution, went over to Amr of Melitene, and founded Argaoun and Amara before Tephrike.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tephrike (Divriği)", "lat": 39.371, "lon": 38.114, "role": "Paulician capital"}, {"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "allied Arab emirate"}, {"name": "Argaous", "lat": 38.95, "lon": 38.2, "role": "Paulician fortress (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "C2-011", "date": "843", "date_end": "847", "date_text": "Consecrated 843–847; archiepiscopate into the reign of Basil I (867–886)", "precision": "year", "title": "Athanasios the Exorcist, metropolitan in the Chrysokephalos", "summary": "Athanasios 'Daimonokatalytes', a Trapezuntine, monk of St Phokas at Kymina and disciple of the metropolitan Nikephoros, was raised 'from bishopric to metropolis' ('ἐξ ἐπισκοπῆς εἰς μητρόπολιν') by Patriarch Methodios and went to the 'metropolitan church of the Chrysokephalos'. Under Basil I he first celebrated the feast of the birthday of St Eugenios (24 June). Chrysanthos rejects the 1600s dating of older lists.", "people": ["Athanasios of Trebizond", "Methodios I of Constantinople", "Basil I", "Nikephoros of Trapezous", "John Chaldes"], "sources_primary": ["Synaxarion of the Church of Constantinople (Athanasios)", "Life of Athanasios the Exorcist"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 217–221; p. 153", "Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 75–77 (the birthday festival story in Lazaropoulos’ Logos: Archbishop Athanasios calms the townspeople)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 320 (St Athanasios the Exorcist, abbot of St Phokas, metropolitan of Trebizond 867-86, kept living in the monastery and restored its katholikon of the Theotokos; the monastery to be sought in the Chotza district)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "That the Chrysokephalos was already the metropolitan church rests on the hagiography. Rosenqvist (1996) tells the story of the first birthday festival differently: the abbot Anthony of St Eugenios paid for it with money from his relatives in Paipert, and Athanasios calmed the townspeople who had been left out; he doubts the story as a whole (C2a-01). Bryer & Winfield (1985) date Athanasios' time as metropolitan to 867–886, the reign of Basil I, rather than from 843–847.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chrysokephalos church (Ortahisar Fatih Mosque), Trabzon (approx.)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.719, "role": "metropolitan church"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "consecration"}]}, {"id": "H3-032", "date": "863", "date_text": "Summer – 3 September 863", "precision": "day", "title": "Umar of Melitene sacks Amisos; he is killed at Lalakaon", "summary": "Umar al-Aqta, emir of Melitene, crossed the Armeniakon and sacked Amisos on the coast. Roman chroniclers say that, angry at the sea for stopping him, he had it lashed (a story borrowed from Xerxes). Petronas then trapped his army by the river Lalakaon (Poson) and killed him on 3 September 863, the turning point of the Arab wars in Asia Minor.", "people": ["Umar al-Aqta", "Petronas", "Nasar", "Michael III"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus IV 25 (Bonn p. 179)", "Genesios IV 15", "Acta Davidis, Symeonis et Georgii 31 (p. 252): the defeat near Poson on 3 September 863", "Pseudo-Symeon 665–666; Leo Grammaticus 238–239; Georgius Monachus Continuatus 825", "al-Tabari, History (vol. 35)", "al-Ya'qubi, History"], "sources_modern": ["Huxley 1975, 'The emperor Michael III and the battle of Bishop's Meadow', GRBS 16, pp. 443–450", "Haldon 2001, The Byzantine Wars, pp. 83–84", "PBE, 'Amr 2' (pbe.kcl.ac.uk/data/D02/F19.htm)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Umar's army is given as 8,000 (al-Ya'qubi) or 40,000 (Genesios, Theophanes Continuatus); Haldon estimates 15,000–20,000. The battlefield has not been identified.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "waypoint 1, Umar's emirate"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.302, "lon": 36.335, "role": "waypoint 2, sacked"}, {"name": "Lalakaon river (Poson)", "lat": 40.95, "lon": 34.9, "role": "waypoint 3, battle (site unidentified; placed near the Halys)"}]}, {"id": "C2a-01", "date": "867", "date_end": "886", "date_text": "Reign of Basil I (867–886), by the account of Lazaropoulos; forgotten after the Seljuk invasions, late 11th c.", "precision": "decade", "title": "The festival of St Eugenios on 24 June and the fair of Trebizond", "summary": "According to John Lazaropoulos (14th c.), under Basil I the martyr revealed in visions that his birthday, 24 June (the feast of John the Baptist), should be kept besides his death-day of 21 January. The abbot Anthony of St Eugenios paid for the first celebration with help from his rich relatives in Paipert, a largely Armenian town, and Archbishop Athanasios calmed the townspeople who had been left out. The midsummer date suited the caravans that brought the monastery’s rents from Paipert over the Pontic passes, closed in January, and competed with the feast of St Orentios at Rhizaion on the same day. The festival became the city’s great fair; government officials attended it until the Seljuk invasions of the late 11th century ended the celebration, which Lazaropoulos says was revived only by Alexios II (1297–1330).", "people": ["Basil I", "Anthony (abbot of St Eugenios)", "Athanasios of Trebizond", "Leo Photeinos", "Alexios II Grand Komnenos", "John (Joseph) Lazaropoulos"], "sources_primary": ["John (Joseph) Lazaropoulos, Logos on the birthday of St Eugenios, Mir. 1 (BHG 611; Rosenqvist 1996); the passage on the festival’s oblivion, Logos lines 196–205"], "sources_modern": ["Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 71–77 (summary and critique of the story; Armenian influence at the monastery in the 9th–10th c.)", "Bryer & Winfield 1978, 'Cities of Heraclius', BMGS 4 (repr. Bryer 1980, I), pp. 28–29 (the feast moved from 21 January to 24 June in the late 9th c.; the caravans via Sourmaina; Orentios at Rhizaion)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 15, 160–161 (instituted under Basil I; officially observed until the end of the 11th c.)", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), pp. 211–212", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 169 (Lazaropoulos' tradition that under Basil I the martyr's birthday was revealed as 24 June; Janin's explanation by the caravans from Paipertes; 24 June also the feast of St Orentios of Rhizaion); p. 223 (the early monastery dependent on annual caravans via Matzouka or Sourmaina to its estates round Paipertes)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Rosenqvist (1996, Swedish) holds that the story as a whole is suspect, above all its revival by Alexios II, of which the monastery's typikon of 1346 says nothing; he accepts as genuine the break caused by the Seljuk invasion and the strong Armenian (Chalcedonian) presence at the monastery. Bryer & Winfield (1978, British), Vryonis (1971, Greek-American) and Sheard (2021, British) accept the institution under Basil I and the end of official observance about 1100. The story overlaps with C2-011, which credits Athanasios with the first celebration.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Monastery of St Eugenios, Trapezous", "lat": 41.003, "lon": 39.718, "role": "festival and relics"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "home of the abbot’s family; the monastery’s estates"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 40.522, "role": "rival feast of St Orentios, 24 June"}]}, {"id": "H3-033", "date": "872", "date_end": "878", "date_text": "872 (or 878); Tephrike razed 878", "precision": "year", "title": "Bathys Ryax: the end of the Paulician state", "summary": "Returning from a raid, Chrysocheir was caught by the strategoi of the Armeniakon and Charsianon at the pass of Bathys Ryax west of Sebasteia; his army was routed and he was killed. Tephrike was taken and razed (878), and many Paulicians were moved to the Balkans and Italy. The frontier of the Pontic themes was secure from the south.", "people": ["Chrysocheir", "Christopher (domestikos)", "Basil I"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus 5 (Vita Basilii) 41–43", "Genesios 4.35–37"], "sources_modern": ["Haldon 2001, The Byzantine Wars, pp. 85–87", "Lemerle 1973", "Garsoïan 1967", "Bryer & Winfield 1978, 'Cities of Heraclius', BMGS 4 (repr. Bryer 1980, I), pp. 21–22 (Bathys Rhyax = the Kalınırmak gorge by Menteşe station, 12 km from Yıldızeli and 25 km from Sebasteia; the battle dated 872 after Genesios)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 27 n. 100, 28 and n. 104 (the aplekton of Bathys Rhyax 'a few kilometers before Sebasteia'; a road from it to Tephrike attested by the aplekta list; Basil I's own campaign against the Paulicians 'probably in 871'; refers to Brown, Bryer and Winfield 1978, pp. 19-22)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The battle is dated 872 (Vasiliev, Lemerle, Treadgold) or 878 (Garsoïan, Haldon). Bryer & Winfield (1978) date it 872 and place Bathys Rhyax at the Kalınırmak gorge near Menteşe station.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bathys Ryax (Kalınırmak pass)", "lat": 39.837, "lon": 36.701, "role": "battle"}, {"name": "Tephrike (Divriği)", "lat": 39.371, "lon": 38.114, "role": "razed 878"}]}, {"id": "C2a-08", "date": "884", "date_text": "884/5 (restoration under Basil I)", "precision": "year", "title": "St Anne’s, the oldest standing church of Trebizond", "summary": "The small church of St Anne (Küçük Ayvasıl) is the only church built before 1204 that still stands in Trebizond; it was restored, or rebuilt, under Basil I in 884/5 (Eastmond 2004). All the others were rebuilt on a larger scale under the Grand Komnenoi. Over its south door is a reused classical relief: Eastmond takes it for part of a sarcophagus, Bryer & Winfield (1985) describe an armed warrior and a flying angel. In the late 14th and early 15th centuries it served as a burial chapel, with portraits and epitaphs of officials, especially churchmen (Bryer & Winfield).", "sources_primary": ["Building inscription of 884/5 (as given by Eastmond 2004)"], "sources_modern": ["Eastmond ed. 2016, Byzantium’s Other Empire: Trebizond, pp. 51–52 (‘only one church built before 1204 survives in the city, that of St. Anne’; ‘the ninth-century church of St. Anne’)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 31 ('restored by Basil I in 885'), p. 33 fig. 10 ('Rebuilt by Basil I in 884/5, this is the oldest surviving church in the city'), pp. 44–45 and fig. 21 (the reused sarcophagus over the south entrance, 884/5)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 218-219 (inscription of AM 6393 = 884/85 on a reused classical relief over the south door: restored under Basil I, Leo VI and Alexander, in the governorship of the protospatharios Alexios; the church probably rebuilt from its foundations; Turkish name Küçük Ayvasıl; painted epitaphs dated 1361/62 to 1413; a mortuary chapel \"in the late fourteenth and early fifteenth centuries\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Eastmond (2004) describes the work of 884/5 both as a restoration and as a rebuilding.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (St Anne’s church, in the city; exact point not taken)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "church"}]}, {"id": "C2-012", "date": "905", "date_text": "Notitiae of Leo VI (c. 901–907) and of the late 10th century", "precision": "decade", "title": "The metropolis of Trapezous and its inland bishoprics", "summary": "In the Notitia of Leo VI, the metropolis of Trapezous, 'of the eparchy of Lazike', has seven suffragan sees: Cheriana, Chamatzour, Chaliou, Paiper, Kerameos, Leriou and Bizanon. A list of the time of Tzimiskes has eighteen entries, seventeen sees in Chrysanthos' count, since Chachaion appears twice; it adds Mananaleos, Zarinakon, Andakou, Mesoune, Chachaion, Sakamou, Chabtzitzin, Chantierz, Olnoute, Phasiane and Sermantzou. A list of about 980 has fifteen. A letter of Patriarch Methodios III of 1670, quoted by Chrysanthos, places Chachaion at Kelkit, Bizana at Tercan, Chantierz at Erzincan, Phasiane at Pasin, Paiper at Bayburt and Leriou at 'Kouazi'. Lazaropoulos calls Cheriana (Şiran), 'between Chaldia and Koloneia', 'the first of the bishoprics of Trapezous'.", "people": ["Leo VI", "John I Tzimiskes"], "sources_primary": ["Notitiae episcopatuum (Leo VI; list of the time of Tzimiskes; list of c. 980)", "Letter of Patriarch Methodios III, 1670"], "sources_modern": ["Gelzer, Notitiae (as used by Chrysanthos)", "Bees (Andaka = Andrapa)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 154–164; pp. 154, 155, 158; p. 163", "Bryer 1977, 'Excursus on Mananalis, Samosata of Armenia and Paulician geography' (repr. Bryer 1980, II), pp. 83–84 (Bizana in Derxene, near Tercan, perhaps from 856; Mananalis a see after 969 that drops out within eleven years; Chantiarte/Chantierz)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 33, 53, 170-171 (Kelkit is an Ottoman administrative creation; Bryer & Winfield place the see of Chachaion/Chalaiou tentatively near Kalur and Aşağı Hayduruk rather than at Kelkit; the see of Cheriana perhaps at Ulu Şiran); p. 35 (Bizana placed by Honigmann near Vican); pp. 313-316 (the see of Leri at Kabaköy in the Leri/Yağmur Dere, moved to Karmout about 1737)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Most of the sees cannot be located with certainty, and several (Chamatzour, Kerameos, Mesoune) not at all. Bryer (1977, British) prefers Chrysanthos' identifications to those of Bees, but places Chantierz south of the 'Mountains of the Devil' (Şeytan Dağları), far south-east of Erzincan, and calls its identification with Erzincan in a Soumela manuscript of 1737 misleading; he locates Mananalis in the southern Tuzlasuyu plain around Tercan. Bryer & Winfield (1985) do not accept Kelkit for Chachaion, suggesting the region of Kalur and Aşağı Hayduruk, and place Leriou at Kabaköy in the Leri valley in Chaldia, where they describe 'probably the oldest surviving church in the Pontos'. The names in Leo VI's list are Χεριάνων, Χαμάτζουρ, Χαλίου, Παΐπερ, Κεραμέως, Λερίου and Βιζάνων; the list of Tzimiskes adds Μανανάλεως, Ζαρινάκων, Ἀνδάκου, Μεσουνῆ, Χαχαίου, Σακάμου, Χαβτζίτζιν, Χαντίερζ, Ὀλνούτην, Φασιανῆς and Σερμάντζου.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "metropolis"}, {"name": "Cheriana (Şiran)", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 39.125, "role": "suffragan see (first)"}, {"name": "Paiper (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.224, "role": "suffragan see"}, {"name": "Chachaion (Kelkit)", "lat": 40.128, "lon": 39.436, "role": "suffragan see (Chrysanthos's identification)"}, {"name": "Bizana (Tercan)", "lat": 39.777, "lon": 40.386, "role": "suffragan see (Chrysanthos's identification)"}, {"name": "Chantierz (Erzincan)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "suffragan see (Chrysanthos's identification)"}, {"name": "Phasiane (Pasinler)", "lat": 39.98, "lon": 41.675, "role": "suffragan see (Chrysanthos's identification)"}, {"name": "Olnoute (Oltu)", "lat": 40.545, "lon": 41.995, "role": "suffragan see (Chrysanthos's identification)"}]}, {"id": "C2-013", "date": "913", "date_end": "914", "date_text": "AM 6422 = 913/914", "precision": "year", "title": "Metropolitan Basil renews the throne of the Chrysokephalos", "summary": "An inscription in the Chrysokephalos records that 'the precious and holy throne was renewed' ('Ἀνεκαινίσθη ὁ τίμιος καὶ ἅγιος θρόνος') under metropolitan Basil in AM 6422 (913/914). Chrysanthos uses it to show that the Chrysokephalos was the metropolitan church by 914. Basil's feast is 7 October.", "people": ["Basil of Trapezous"], "sources_primary": ["Inscription in the Chrysokephalos, AM 6422"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 221–222; pp. 380–381", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 238, 240 (the inscription of 913/14 on Metropolitan Basil's restored throne, found in the floor in 1877, now presumed lost; the earliest evidence that the Chrysokephalos was the cathedral)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chrysokephalos church (Ortahisar Fatih Mosque), Trabzon (approx.)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.719, "role": "inscription"}]}, {"id": "H3-034", "date": "923", "date_text": "C. 923", "precision": "year", "title": "Bardas Boilas, strategos of Chaldia, rebels", "summary": "Bardas Boilas, strategos of Chaldia, rebelled against Romanos I with local magnates; John Kourkouas suppressed the revolt. It is the first of several attempts by the governors of Trapezous to act on their own, which culminate in the Gabras family's rule after 1075.", "people": ["Bardas Boilas", "Romanos I Lekapenos", "John Kourkouas"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus 6 (Romanos I)", "Skylitzes, Romanos I"], "sources_modern": ["Runciman 1929, The Emperor Romanus Lecapenus", "Bryer 1980, Variorum", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 328 (John Kourkouas suppressed a Chaldian revolt in 923 and installed his brother Theophilos as strategos); p. 316 and nn. 9-10 (Bardas Boilas, strategos of Chaldia to 923: with Adrian the Chaldian and Tatzakes he seized Bayburt and was arrested in 923 for conspiring against Romanos Lekapenos; Theophanes Continuatus, Bonn ed., 404; George Continuatus, Bonn ed., 896-97; Runciman 1929, 70-71, 135; replaced by Theophilos Kourkouas, 923/24-40)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985) date the revolt to 923 and add that Boilas, with Adrian the Chaldian and Tatzakes, seized Bayburt; John Kourkouas then installed his brother Theophilos as strategos (923/4–940).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "seat of the rebel strategos"}, {"name": "Paipert", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "inland Chaldia (place of the revolt not specified)"}]}, {"id": "H3-035", "date": "934", "date_end": "949", "date_text": "934 (Melitene); 939/40 (Sayf al-Dawla's raid); 949 (Theodosiopolis)", "precision": "year", "title": "The frontier moves east; Pontus becomes an interior province", "summary": "John Kourkouas took Melitene (934) and the Romans took Theodosiopolis (949). Sayf al-Dawla's raid of 939/40, which reached the theme of Koloneia, was the last great Muslim raid on the region. From the mid-10th century until the 1050s the Pontic themes lay well behind the frontier, which favoured the growth of trade at Trapezous and of large estates inland.", "people": ["John Kourkouas", "Sayf al-Dawla"], "sources_primary": ["Theophanes Continuatus 6", "Constantine VII, De administrando imperio 45", "Ibn Zafir and other Arabic sources in Canard 1951"], "sources_modern": ["Whittow 1996, The Making of Orthodox Byzantium, 600–1025", "Canard 1951, Histoire de la dynastie des H'amdanides", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 146 (Sayf ad-Dawla destroyed the villages round Koloneia in 939-40)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "taken 934"}, {"name": "Koloneia", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "target of Sayf al-Dawla, 939/40"}, {"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "taken 949"}]}, {"id": "H3-036", "date": "943", "date_end": "977", "date_text": "C. 943–947 (al-Mas'udi); c. 950 (al-Istakhri); c. 977 (Ibn Hawqal)", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Muslim geographers on the fairs of Trebizond", "summary": "Al-Mas'udi (about 943–947) describes Trebizond as a market town on the Black Sea where, in Sheard's English after Pellat's translation, 'there is a fair once a year at which merchants assemble from all nations: Muslims, Rūm, Armenians and others from the country of Kashak'; the Kashak, probably Qipchaqs, sailed constantly to the city. Al-Istakhri (about 950) wrote that 'all the textiles of Greek manufacture, all the brocades imported into Muslim territory are carried through Trebizond'; al-Muqaddasi (late 10th century) called it the most important Muslim trade colony in the empire; al-Idrisi (12th century) still knew it as a trading town. Lazaropoulos, in the 14th century, ties the chief fair to the feast of St Eugenios (C2a-01). Sheard (2021) argues that the Seljuk wars only interrupted this trade about 1100, and that it had recovered by 1205, when Ibn al-Athir describes merchants from Syria, Iraq, Mosul, Rus' and the Qipchaqs cut off by the Seljuk siege.", "people": ["al-Mas'udi", "al-Istakhri", "Ibn Hawqal", "al-Muqaddasi", "al-Idrisi"], "sources_primary": ["al-Mas'udi, Muruj al-dhahab, ed. Barbier de Meynard II, pp. 46–47; ed. Pellat (Beirut 1966) II, pp. 230–231", "Ibn Hawqal, Surat al-ard, ed. Kramers (BGA 2), p. 344; tr. Kramers & Wiet, Configuration de la terre II, p. 337", "al-Istakhri, Masalik al-mamalik (BGA 1)"], "sources_modern": ["Polgár, 'Notes on the role of Alania in international trade', Chronica (Szeged), n. 14", "Bryer 1980, Variorum", "Serjeant 1972, Islamic Textiles, pp. 63, 213", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), pp. 200–204, 207, 210–212 (al-Muqaddasi, al-Istakhri, al-Mas‘udi, al-Idrisi, Ibn al-Athir; the trade of Trebizond, 10th–12th c.)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 15–16 (several fairs a year, the panegyris of St Eugenios the chief; merchants of many nations)", "Bryer 1980, The Empire of Trebizond and the Pontos (Variorum), Introduction, p. i (commerce counted for less than farming)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether there was one fair a year or several is uncertain: 'a fair once a year' in al-Mas'udi as quoted by Sheard (2021) from Pellat's translation, 'several market fairs held each year' in Vryonis (1971), citing the Barbier de Meynard edition. Sheard dates Ibn Hawqal's figure of 10 quintals of gold a year to the early 13th century, which cannot be right for a 10th-century author; it is not used. On the trade of Trebizond in the 11th and 12th centuries: Jacoby (Israeli) sees a long decline from the late 10th century, as the eastern trade moved to Fatimid Egypt; Sheard (2021, British) a temporary interruption by the Seljuk wars about 1100, then recovery; Vryonis (1971, Greek-American) a break in the peace and prosperity of the emporium, shown by the interruption of the festival; and Bryer (1980, British) holds that commerce counted for less in Pontic life than farming and herding.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Atrabazunda)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "trading port and fair"}, {"name": "Monastery of St Eugenios, Trapezous", "lat": 41.003, "lon": 39.718, "role": "patron saint's shrine (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "C2a-02", "date": "988", "date_end": "989", "date_text": "988–989 (Sheard: 987–988), during the revolt of Bardas Phokas", "precision": "year", "title": "Trebizond’s grain ships feed Constantinople; an Iberian army marches on the city", "summary": "During the revolt of Bardas Phokas, Basil II ordered the towns of the Black Sea coast ‘as far as Trebizond’ to ship grain to the capital, and many ships came from Trebizond and the places round it. Phokas’s commander Delphinas, who guarded the Bosporus, could not stop them and urged Phokas to ravage Trebizond. Two generals ‘of Persarmenia’, Pankratios and Tzourbaneles, marched on the city through Paipert and camped by the ‘Narrow Pass’ while the Trapezuntines fortified their villages; in the story St Eugenios appeared to Pankratios with news of Phokas’s death (at Abydos, 989), and the army turned back.", "people": ["Basil II", "Bardas Phokas", "Delphinas", "Pankratios (perhaps Bagrat)", "Tzourbaneles (perhaps Chordvaneli)"], "sources_primary": ["John (Joseph) Lazaropoulos, Synopsis of the miracles, Mir. 1, lines 101–140 (Rosenqvist 1996, text and translation pp. 250–255)"], "sources_modern": ["Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 49–50 (the episode is Lazaropoulos’ own insertion into a summary of Zonaras), 253 (translation), 406–407 (comm. on lines 113–114)", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), pp. 111, 152"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The story is told only by a 14th-century hagiographer, who inserted it into his summary of Zonaras (Rosenqvist). Rosenqvist suggests that the generals may not be real persons, or may be Chordvaneli II, a commander captured in 988/9, and the patrikios Bagrat II; Iberian troops of David of Tao had supported Phokas. Sheard (2021) uses the grain order as evidence for grain shipments from Trebizond and dates it 987–988. The 'Narrow Pass' has not been located.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "destination of the grain ships"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "grain port; threatened"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "route of the Iberian army"}]}, {"id": "H3-037", "date": "1001", "date_text": "979 (aid against Skleros); early 1001 (death of David)", "precision": "year", "title": "David of Tao's inheritance: Rome annexes Tao", "summary": "The Georgian Bagratid kouropalates David III of Tao helped Basil II against the rebel Bardas Skleros (979) and was granted lands round Theodosiopolis for life; after he backed Bardas Phokas (987) he made Basil his heir. On his death in early 1001 Basil annexed Tao and the lands to Lake Van as the theme of Iberia, bringing the Roman border to the Georgian kingdom of Bagrat III just south-east of Chaldia.", "people": ["David III of Tao", "Basil II", "Bardas Skleros", "Bardas Phokas", "Bagrat III"], "sources_primary": ["Skylitzes, Basil II", "Yahya of Antioch", "Stephen of Taron (Asoghik) 3"], "sources_modern": ["Holmes 2005, Basil II and the Governance of Empire", "Toumanoff 1963, Studies in Christian Caucasian History"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Some date the annexation 1000, others 1001.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tao (Oltisi area)", "lat": 40.541, "lon": 41.998, "role": "David's principality, annexed 1001"}, {"name": "Theodosiopolis", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "granted to David for life"}]}, {"id": "H3-038", "date": "1021", "date_end": "1022", "date_text": "Autumn 1021 – 1022", "precision": "month", "title": "Basil II winters at Trapezous during his Georgian war", "summary": "Basil II invaded Georgia, won a costly battle at Shirimni by Lake Palakatsio (11 September 1021) and wintered at Trapezous. There, in December 1021, Senekerim-Hovhannes of Vaspurakan ceded his kingdom; Armenian sources say the will of John-Smbat of Ani leaving Ani to the empire was also handed to Basil at Trapezous. After a victory at Svindax in 1022, George I ceded Tao, Kola, Artaan and Javakheti and gave his son Bagrat as a hostage. For one winter Trapezous was the seat of the emperor. The 14th-c. Lazaropoulos makes St Eugenios help Basil in this Iberian campaign and Basil a benefactor of the martyr’s monastery (Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 49, 409).", "people": ["Basil II", "George I of Georgia", "Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni", "John-Smbat III of Ani", "Bagrat IV"], "sources_primary": ["Skylitzes, Basil II (Thurn ed., pp. 366–367)", "Yahya of Antioch", "Aristakes Lastivertsi, History 3:1 (Basil wintered 'in temperate Pontus'; Trebizond not named) and 3:8 (Senekerim gives his inheritance, Vaspurakan, to Basil and receives Sebasteia; no place or date) (Classical Armenian text with English, historians.armeniancathedral.org, t12Last1_3)", "John Xiphilinos, Miracles of St Eugenios (Basil and the saint)", "John (Joseph) Lazaropoulos, Synopsis of the miracles, Mir. 1, lines c. 190–240 (Rosenqvist 1996)"], "sources_modern": ["Holmes 2005, Basil II", "Bryer 1980, Variorum", "Miller 1926, Trebizond, p. 30", "Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 49 and 409–411 (comm. on Synopsis lines 195, 219): the winter 1021/22 at Trebizond is told by Lazaropoulos and by oriental sources, not by the Greek historians", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), p. 152", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 169, 223 (Basil II rebuilt the monastery of St Eugenios in 1021-22; Xiphilinos would have seen him at Trebizond in 1021/22)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The link with St Eugenios is in Lazaropoulos (14th century), not in Xiphilinos. Rosenqvist (1996) places the battle at Lake Palakatsio in March 1021, and the stay at Trebizond after it; others give 11 September 1021.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Shirimni (Lake Palakatsio, Çıldır)", "lat": 41.12, "lon": 43.22, "role": "battle, 11 September 1021 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Oltisi (Oltu)", "lat": 40.541, "lon": 41.998, "role": "burned by George I in retreat"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "imperial winter quarters 1021/2"}]}, {"id": "H3-039", "date": "1021", "date_end": "1065", "date_text": "1021/2 (Vaspurakan); 1045 (Ani); 1064/5 (Kars)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Armenian kings and their people resettled in Sebasteia and Cappadocia", "summary": "As the empire annexed the Armenian kingdoms, their rulers received lands inside it: Senekerim of Vaspurakan got Sebasteia, Larissa and Abara (1021/2) and came with a large following; Gagik II of Ani got estates in Cappadocia (1045); Gagik-Abas of Kars got Tzamandos, Larissa, Amaseia and Komana (1064/5). Armenian soldiers, nobles and peasants spread through Sebasteia, Cappadocia and the southern edge of Pontus, strengthening an Armenian presence that already went back to the 7th–10th centuries.", "people": ["Senekerim-Hovhannes Artsruni", "Gagik II of Ani", "Gagik-Abas of Kars", "Constantine IX", "Constantine X"], "sources_primary": ["Matthew of Edessa, Chronicle, Part 1 (Senekerim's move to Sebasteia)", "Aristakes Lastivertsi, History", "Skylitzes"], "sources_modern": ["Dédéyan 2003, Les Arméniens entre Grecs, Musulmans et Croisés", "Garsoïan 1998, 'The problem of Armenian integration into the Byzantine Empire', in Ahrweiler & Laiou eds., Studies on the Internal Diaspora", "Garsoïan 1997, in Hovannisian (ed.), The Armenian People from Ancient to Modern Times, vol. 1, p. 190", "Holmes 2005, Basil II and the Governance of Empire, pp. 483–484", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 54–55 (Senekerim and his son David with 14,000 men; Gagik-Abas of Kars given Tzamandos, Larissa, Amaseia and Komana)", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 167 (Gagik of Kars installed in Komana and Amaseia, 1064)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The numbers who moved are unknown. The 14,000 retainers of Senekerim are given by Garsoïan (1997) and by Vryonis (1971), whose notes cite Honigmann, Grousset, Michael the Syrian and Aristakes. Armenian chroniclers blame Roman policy for stripping the frontier of its defenders, and Greek writers of the 1070s blame Armenian disloyalty; Garsoïan and Dédéyan discuss both. Bryer (1970, British) holds that inner Pontus, the Lykos valley, received so many Armenian refugees in the 11th century that it had a higher proportion of Armenians than of Greeks 'until this century'; this is his judgement, not a count.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "seat of the Artsruni after 1021/2"}, {"name": "Van (Vaspurakan)", "lat": 38.5, "lon": 43.38, "role": "ceded kingdom"}, {"name": "Ani", "lat": 40.507, "lon": 43.573, "role": "annexed 1045"}, {"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.098, "role": "ceded 1064/5"}, {"name": "Caesarea in Cappadocia", "lat": 38.722, "lon": 35.487, "role": "area of Gagik II's estates"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "estates of Gagik-Abas of Kars (1064/5)"}, {"name": "Komana Pontike", "lat": 40.357, "lon": 36.625, "role": "estates of Gagik-Abas of Kars (1064/5)"}]}, {"id": "C2-014", "date": "1027-11", "date_end": "1054-06", "date_text": "November 1027 and January 1028 (Konstantinos); June 1054 (Leon)", "precision": "month", "title": "Metropolitans of Trapezous at the patriarchal synods of 1027–1028 and 1054", "summary": "Konstantinos of Trapezous sat in the patriarchal synods of November 1027 and January 1028 (AM 6536), and Leon in the synod of June 1054, the year of the break with Rome. Chrysanthos uses the signatures to fix these two metropolitans.", "people": ["Konstantinos of Trapezous", "Leon of Trapezous", "Michael Keroularios"], "sources_primary": ["Synodal acts of 1027–1028 and 1054 (Rhalles–Potles; Mansi)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 201–204"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "synods"}]}, {"id": "H3-040", "date": "1059", "date_end": "1068", "date_text": "1054 (Paipert), 1057 (Koloneia), 1059 (Sebasteia); 1067–1068 (Caesarea, Neocaesarea)", "precision": "year", "title": "Turkish raids before Manzikert", "summary": "Turkmen raiders sacked Sebasteia (1059), Caesarea with the shrine of St Basil (1067) and Neocaesarea (1068). Romanos IV, marching from Sebasteia, pursued the raiders of Neocaesarea and recovered prisoners and booty. The interior of Pontus was again a war zone, a decade before Manzikert. The Pontic valleys had been hit earlier still: Paipert was raided in 1054 and Koloneia in 1057 (Vryonis 1971, p. 162), and the caravan towns that fed Trebizond’s trade fell one by one: Artze sacked in 1049, Theodosiopolis occupied c. 1071, Ardanoutzin burned in 1080 (Sheard 2021, pp. 210–211).", "people": ["Romanos IV Diogenes", "Afshin (Turkish commander)"], "sources_primary": ["Attaleiates, History (Romanos IV's campaign of 1068)", "Matthew of Edessa, Chronicle 2 (Sebasteia 1059)", "Aristakes Lastivertsi, History ch. 16", "Matthew of Edessa, trans. Dulaurier, pp. 111–113 (Sebasteia)", "Attaliates (Bonn) 93–94, 105", "Skylitzes Continuatus (Cedrenus, Bonn II) 606, 661, 684–685"], "sources_modern": ["Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor", "Beihammer 2017, Byzantium and the Emergence of Muslim-Turkish Anatolia", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 94–95 (Sebasteia 1059: the emir Samuh, eight days; Caesarea and St Basil’s shrine; Neocaesarea sacked the next year while Romanos was in Lykandos), p. 162 (Paipert raided 1054, Koloneia 1057, Neocaesarea sacked 1068)", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), pp. 210–211 (the caravan towns of Trebizond lost: Artze sacked 1049, Theodosiopolis occupied c. 1071, Ardanoutzin burned 1080)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 108 (Neokaisareia \"first sacked by Affin in about 1068\")"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "sacked 1059"}, {"name": "Caesarea in Cappadocia", "lat": 38.722, "lon": 35.487, "role": "sacked 1067"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "sacked 1068"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "raided 1054"}, {"name": "Koloneia (Şebinkarahisar)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "raided 1057"}]}, {"id": "C2a-03", "date": "1064", "date_end": "1075", "date_text": "Born at Trebizond c. 1010–1013; Miracles written after 1028; patriarch 1064–1075 (died 2 August 1075)", "precision": "year", "title": "John Xiphilinos of Trebizond, patriarch of Constantinople", "summary": "John Xiphilinos, born at Trebizond c. 1010–1013, wrote as a young man, before leaving for Constantinople, a collection of ten miracles of St Eugenios and a new Passion of the martyr; they record events in the city in his own and his family’s lifetime. He rose to be patriarch of Constantinople (John VIII, 1064–1075), as Anthimos of Trapezous had been in 535 (C2-007).", "people": ["John VIII Xiphilinos"], "sources_primary": ["John Xiphilinos, Miracles of St Eugenios (BHG 610; Rosenqvist 1996)", "John Xiphilinos, Passion of St Eugenios (BHG 609z)", "Michael Psellos, Encomium of Xiphilinos"], "sources_modern": ["Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 28–30 (date of birth after Bones; the Miracles written at Trebizond after 1028 and probably before 1030)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 169, 300 (John Xiphilinos, a native of Trebizond, patriarch 1064-75, rewrote the Lives of the Four Saints of Trebizond)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "He was born about 1010–1013 or somewhat earlier (Bones, as cited by Rosenqvist).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "birthplace; where the Miracles were written"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "patriarchal see"}]}, {"id": "H3-041", "date": "1071", "date_text": "26 August 1071", "precision": "day", "title": "Manzikert", "summary": "Alp Arslan defeated and captured Romanos IV at Manzikert. The battle itself cost relatively few lives, but the civil wars that followed left the eastern themes without defence, and within fifteen years Turkish emirs held most of the Pontic interior.", "people": ["Romanos IV Diogenes", "Alp Arslan", "Andronikos Doukas"], "sources_primary": ["Attaleiates, History", "Psellos, Chronographia 7", "Nikephoros Bryennios, Historiae 1.13–17", "Aristakes Lastivertsi", "Ibn al-Athir"], "sources_modern": ["Hillenbrand 2007, Turkish Myth and Muslim Symbol: The Battle of Manzikert", "Haldon 2001, The Byzantine Wars", "Vryonis 1971", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 98–105 (the campaign and battle) and 160 (two months’ rations issued at Theodosiopolis for an ‘uninhabited land’)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Older views made the battle a single catastrophe; Cahen, Vryonis and Beihammer stress the civil wars after it.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Manzikert (Malazgirt)", "lat": 39.145, "lon": 42.54, "role": "battlefield"}]}, {"id": "H3-042", "date": "1073", "date_end": "1075", "date_text": "1073–1075", "precision": "year", "title": "Roussel de Bailleul at Amaseia; the young Alexios Komnenos takes him", "summary": "The Norman mercenary leader Roussel de Bailleul broke with the emperor and seized the Armeniakon, ruling from round Amaseia, where the people welcomed his protection against the Turks. Before him, in 1069, the Frankish captain Crispin had made himself master of the fortress of Koloneia, which controlled the Pontic alum mines, and Roussel succeeded him on his death in 1073 (Bryer 1970). Vryonis (1971) adds that Roussel raided Amaseia, Neocaesarea and other towns and forced them to pay their taxes to him. The young general Alexios Komnenos paid the Turkish emir Tutach to hand Roussel over, then calmed the Amaseians, who wanted him freed, by pretending to blind him. Alexios restored imperial authority for a moment (1075), but the Turks soon overran the region.", "people": ["Roussel de Bailleul", "Alexios Komnenos", "Tutach", "Michael VII Doukas", "Crispin (Frankish mercenary leader)"], "sources_primary": ["Anna Komnene, Alexiad 1.1.3–1.3.4", "Nikephoros Bryennios, Historiae 2.17–24", "Attaleiates, History"], "sources_modern": ["Beihammer 2017, p. 212", "Haldon 1999, Warfare, State and Society, pp. 91–92", "Shepard 1973, 'The uses of the Franks in eleventh-century Byzantium', Anglo-Norman Studies", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 106–107 (Roussel withdraws to the Armeniakon, drives out the Turks, raids Amaseia and Neocaesarea and makes the towns pay him taxes)", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 167 (Crispin at Koloneia, 1069–1073; Roussel at Amaseia from 1073)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 147 (in 1069 Koloneia was in the hands of the Norman-Italian mercenary captain Crispin)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "Roussel's base; handed over to Alexios"}, {"name": "Koloneia (Şebinkarahisar)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "held by Crispin, 1069–1073"}]}, {"id": "H3-043", "date": "1075", "date_end": "1098", "date_text": "C. 1075 (or the 1080s) – 1098 (or 1099)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Theodore Gabras takes Trapezous and rules it as his own", "summary": "Theodore Gabras, a Chaldian officer (in 1067 already patrikios and topoteretes, as a gospel book he commissioned records), recovered Trapezous from the Turks and, in Anna Komnene's words, held it 'as a prize that had fallen to his own lot'. Alexios I recognised him as doux of Chaldia. His seals call him doux and sebastos and invoke St Theodore, and a local coinage of Trapezous of c. 1080–1110 with St Theodore is linked to his rule. He held the coast from round Kerasous to Rhizaion, the inland passes, and at times Paipert. Chrysanthos (1933, p. 53) dates the Seljuk capture of Trapezous that Gabras undid to c. 1074, noting Fallmerayer's view (after Hayton, c. 1300) that only the suburbs fell, and takes the city to be free again by 1075, since Psellos's encomium of Patriarch John Xiphilinos (died 2 August 1075) speaks of it as free. Bryer (1970, p. 175) separates two steps: Gabras retook Trebizond from the Turks in 1075, with local Chaldian troops, after an occupation of no more than three years; he then retired to Constantinople, and Alexios I (emperor from 1081) made him duke of Chaldia to get him out of the capital. As duke he defended Koloneia and Neocaesarea against the emir Danishmend and Trebizond against the Georgians, and retook Paipert.", "people": ["Theodore Gabras", "Alexios I Komnenos", "Irene (Gabras' first wife)"], "sources_primary": ["Anna Komnene, Alexiad 8.9.1–7", "Seals of Theodore Gabras (Dumbarton Oaks; Cheynet)", "Gospel of Theodore Gabras, 1067 (St Petersburg)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades', University of Birmingham Historical Journal 12, pp. 164–187", "Dunn, Bryer & Nesbitt 2003, 'Theodore Gabras, Duke of Chaldia (+1098) and the Gabrades', in Avramea, Laiou & Chrysos eds., Byzantium, State and Society, pp. 51–70", "Bendall 1977, Numismatic Chronicle, pp. 126–136", "Feldman 2018, BMGS (sigillography and autonomy)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 53", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), pp. 168, 175 (recaptured Trebizond from the Turks in 1075, after an occupation of no more than three years; later appointed duke by Alexios I)", "Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic exception', DOP 29 (repr. Bryer 1980, V), p. 117 (the Gabrades independent rulers of Chaldia from the 1070s to the 1140s)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 160–161 (Trebizond and Sinope briefly held by the Turks, retaken in the late 11th c.)", "Eastmond ed. 2016, Byzantium’s Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 301 (\"From St. Theodore Gabras to Constantine Gabras, Gabrades ruled Chaldia from about 1075 to after 1140\")", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 108 (Neokaisareia back in Greek hands when St Theodore Gabras defended it in the 1080s; he died in 1098)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "His rule is dated from about 1075 (Bryer) or from the 1080s (Treadgold, who also dates his death to 1099). The two dates refer to different steps: the recapture of the city (1075) and his appointment as duke by Alexios I (1081 or later). How independent he was is debated: Bryer speaks of a 'personal fiefdom', others of a loyal but distant governor. Whether the Turks took the city about 1074, or only its suburbs, is also uncertain: Chrysanthos says the city, Fallmerayer, after Hayton, the suburbs.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Gabras' capital"}, {"name": "Kerasous", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.389, "role": "western limit of the domain (approximate)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 40.522, "role": "eastern coast of the domain"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "inland outpost (held at times)"}]}, {"id": "H3-044", "date": "1075", "date_end": "1085", "date_text": "C. 1075–1085", "precision": "decade", "title": "Danishmend Gazi takes Sebasteia, Neocaesarea, Tokat and Amaseia", "summary": "A Turkish commander known as Danishmend took the inland cities of Pontus and Armenia Minor (Sebasteia, Neocaesarea, Dokeia (Tokat), Komana, Amaseia, Euchaita) and founded the Danishmendid emirate, which held them until 1174–78. The Greek-speaking Christians of these towns stayed, now under Muslim rule, while the coast stayed Roman. The dates are uncertain; Michael the Syrian has him ruling 'Cappadocia' in 1085. Their coins bore Greek legends, with the title ‘Grand Melik of All Romania and the East’ (Bryer 1970, p. 170). By the crusade of 1101 they also held Paipert and Koloneia, which Theodore Gabras retook for a time (Vryonis 1971, p. 162).", "people": ["Danishmend Gazi"], "sources_primary": ["Michael the Syrian, Chronicle 15 (tr. Chabot III)", "Matthew of Edessa, Chronicle", "Niketas Choniates (on his descent)"], "sources_modern": ["Cahen 1968, Pre-Ottoman Turkey", "Yazici 1993, 'Danishmendids', Encyclopaedia Iranica, pp. 654–655", "Beihammer 2017", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 114–115 (Danishmend’s domain: Sivas, Amasya, Komana, Tokat, Niksar, Çankırı, Ankara, Malatya), 162 (by 1101 the Danishmendids held Paipert, Koloneia, Neocaesarea, Dokeia, Komana, Amaseia, Euchaita, Gangra; Gabras retook Paipert and Koloneia for a time)", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), pp. 167–170 (infiltration rather than conquest, c. 1072 to after 1106; the Danishmendid coin title ‘Grand Melik of All Romania and the East’ in Greek)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 108 (by 1098 Neokaisareia had probably fallen to the emir Danishmend, \"who made it his capital\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Danishmend's origin (a Turkmen; of Armenian descent, as Matthew of Edessa says; or 'Arsacid', as Choniates has it) and the order and dates of his conquests are unknown. The Turkish epic of his deeds, the Danishmendname, was written down in the 14th century. On the conquest and settlement of inner Pontus: Bryer (1970, 1975, British) holds that north-east Anatolia was not conquered by one army, but contested piecemeal between about 1072 and after 1106 by Turkmen groups and local Christian lords, castle by castle and pasture by pasture, and that the Gabras family and the Danishmendids had more in common with each other than with Constantinople or Konya; Vryonis (1971, Greek-American) holds that the sacks, flight and breakdown of the church in the interior began a long decline of Greek Christianity there, with conversion gradual and largely later; Cahen (1968, French) calls the Danishmendname 'an intricate blend of genuine memories and legendary versions' (see also C2a-06).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "Danishmendid capital (later)"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "Danishmendid seat"}, {"name": "Dokeia (Tokat)", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "taken"}, {"name": "Komana Pontike", "lat": 40.357, "lon": 36.625, "role": "taken"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "taken; possibly the first capital"}, {"name": "Euchaita (Avkat)", "lat": 40.57, "lon": 35.22, "role": "taken (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H3-045", "date": "1086", "date_end": "1092", "date_text": "C. 1086–1092", "precision": "decade", "title": "Sinope recovered from the emir Karatekin", "summary": "A Turkish emir, Karatekin, had seized Sinope. In Anna Komnene's account the sultan's envoy Siaous, sent to remove Turkish governors from the coastal cities, handed Sinope over to Alexios instead and became a Christian, and Alexios' general Constantine Dalassenos took over the city. The episode shows how close the Turks came to holding the whole Pontic coast.", "people": ["Karatekin", "Siaous", "Constantine Dalassenos", "Alexios I Komnenos", "Malik-Shah"], "sources_primary": ["Anna Komnene, Alexiad 6.9.3–6", "Anna Komnene, Alexiad (ed. Leib) II, 64–66 (= 6.9)"], "sources_modern": ["Beihammer 2017", "Frankopan 2012, The First Crusade: The Call from the East", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 114–115 (Karatekin held Sinope and the neighbouring towns briefly; restored to the emperor by 1085), 161 (Sinope and Trebizond both briefly held by the Turks)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 70-71 (Karatekin, one of Malikshah's emirs, set up an ephemeral state at Sinope in 1081; a Christian convert betrayed him to Alexios I and handed the port to Constantine Dalassenos; the treasury gold in Sinope left untouched; Karatekin desecrated the church of the Panagia)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date and the sequence are argued; Frankopan dates Alexios' eastern recoveries earlier than most. Bryer & Winfield (1985) date Karatekin's seizure of Sinope to 1081, citing Anna Komnene; Vryonis (1971) has Sinope back in imperial hands 'by 1085'; Anna places the handover after the death of the sultan Sulayman (1086).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "taken by Karatekin, recovered"}]}, {"id": "H3-046", "date": "1091", "date_text": "1091", "precision": "year", "title": "Gabras and his son Gregory: a hostage in Constantinople", "summary": "Alexios kept Theodore Gabras' son Gregory at court as a hostage and betrothed him to a daughter of Isaac Komnenos. When the betrothal was broken off, Theodore tried to take Gregory home by sea, but imperial ships overtook him and he gave the boy up. Gregory later tried to escape to his father and was imprisoned at Philippopolis. About the same time Theodore married a princess 'from Alania', identified by Toumanoff as the Georgian Bagratid Mariam. He had put the boy on a merchant ship at the Bosporus; the imperial ships caught them beyond Aiginos, near Karambis (Alexiad, as quoted by Sheard 2021, p. 152), evidence of ordinary shipping between the capital and Trebizond.", "people": ["Theodore Gabras", "Gregory Gabras", "Alexios I Komnenos", "Isaac Komnenos (sebastokrator)", "Mariam of Alania"], "sources_primary": ["Anna Komnene, Alexiad 8.9.1–7"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970, pp. 164–187", "Garland & Rapp 2006, in Garland ed., Byzantine Women, pp. 94–95", "Toumanoff 1963", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), p. 152 (the escape by merchant ship, quoting the Alexiad)", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), pp. 175–176 (imperial letter of May 1091, Dölger, Regesten 1161; Gregory taken to Karambis)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The identity of Theodore's Alan wife with a Georgian princess is Toumanoff's hypothesis.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "where Gregory was held"}, {"name": "Philippopolis", "lat": 42.146, "lon": 24.75, "role": "where Gregory was imprisoned"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Theodore's seat"}, {"name": "Karambis (Cape Kerempe), approx.", "lat": 42.02, "lon": 33.34, "role": "where the imperial ships overtook Gabras and his son"}]}, {"id": "H3-047", "date": "1098", "date_text": "1098 (or 1099)", "precision": "year", "title": "Theodore Gabras killed by the Turks; venerated as a martyr", "summary": "While the crusaders besieged Antioch, Theodore Gabras was fighting the Turks in the east. He was captured on a campaign towards Theodosiopolis and put to death, according to his cult for refusing to give up his faith. The Church commemorates him as a martyr on 2 October. His family kept Chaldia as a quasi-hereditary command for two generations. Vryonis (1971, p. 360) names his captor as the emir Ali (Amirales), in the region of Paipert and Erzurum; his skull, made into a drinking cup, was brought back secretly to Trebizond by Constantine Gabras between 1115 and 1140 and became the centre of his cult. His monastery stood in the eastern suburb of Trebizond until Alexios III gave it to the Venetians in 1364 (Bryer 1970, p. 170).", "people": ["Theodore Gabras", "Amir Ali (Amirales)", "Constantine Gabras"], "sources_primary": ["Synaxarion notice of St Theodore Gabras (2 October)", "Anna Komnene, Alexiad (passing references)"], "sources_modern": ["Dunn, Bryer & Nesbitt 2003", "Bryer 1980, Variorum, p. 47", "ODB 1991, 'Gabras', p. 812", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), pp. 170, 175 (killed fighting the Turks at Theodosiopolis in 1098; martyr in the 12th c., saint by the 14th; feast 2 October; his monastery in the eastern suburb given to the Venetians in 1364)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, p. 360 and n. 22 (captured c. 1098 by the emir Ali (Amirales) in the region of Paipert–Erzurum; tortured for refusing to convert; skull returned to Trebizond by Constantine Gabras between 1115 and 1140)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 300 (St Theodore Gabras martyred by the Turks in 1098; a tradition, perhaps a late one, makes him a native of Atra, now Dörtkonak)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Most works date his death to 1098 (Bryer 1970; Vryonis 1971; Dunn, Bryer & Nesbitt); Treadgold (1997) has 1099. Vryonis, after the synaxarion, names his captor as the emir Ali rather than the Danishmendids. The story of his torture comes from the martyr's cult texts.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Theodosiopolis region", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "area of his last campaign (approximate)"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.256, "lon": 40.224, "role": "fortress taken in his last campaigns"}]}, {"id": "H3-048", "date": "1100", "date_end": "1103", "date_text": "August 1100 – 1103", "precision": "month", "title": "Bohemond of Antioch a prisoner at Neocaesarea", "summary": "Danishmend Gazi Gümüshtigin captured Bohemond, prince of Antioch and a leader of the First Crusade, near Melitene in August 1100 and held him at Neocaesarea (Niksar) until he was ransomed in 1103. Alexios I offered 260,000 dinars to have him handed over; the ransom finally paid is given as 100,000–130,000 dinars, raised with help from the Armenian lord Kogh Vasil (Matthew of Edessa, as cited by MacEvitt 2010 and Morton 2020). The captive drew the crusade of 1101 into Pontus.", "people": ["Bohemond I of Antioch", "Gümüshtigin Gazi", "Alexios I Komnenos", "Kogh Vasil"], "sources_primary": ["Albert of Aachen, Historia Ierosolimitana 7.27–29 and 9.33–36", "Matthew of Edessa, Chronicle 2", "Anna Komnene, Alexiad 11.11"], "sources_modern": ["Runciman 1952, A History of the Crusades II", "Asbridge 2000, The Creation of the Principality of Antioch", "Barber 2012, p. 78", "Morton 2020, p. 83", "MacEvitt 2010, p. 86", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 176 (Bohemond held at Neocaesarea 1100–1103; Theophylact of Ohrid suggests Gregory Gabras-Taronites helped negotiate the ransom)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 108 (Neokaisareia, capital of the emir Danishmend by 1098; Bohemund imprisoned there from 1100 to 1103; the Lombard crusade of 1101 failed to reach it)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figures are taken from modern historians (MacEvitt 2010; Morton 2020), after Matthew of Edessa.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "where Bohemond was captured"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "place of captivity"}]}, {"id": "H3-049", "date": "1101", "date_text": "23 June 1101 (Ankyra) – August 1101 (Mersivan)", "precision": "month", "title": "The crusade of 1101 destroyed at Mersivan", "summary": "A large Lombard-led army, with Raymond of Toulouse and a Roman escort of Pechenegs under Tzitas, took Ankyra (23 June 1101) and returned it to Alexios, failed before Gangra, took Kastamon, and then, against Raymond's advice, marched east across the Halys to free Bohemond at Neocaesarea. Kilij Arslan and the Danishmendids surrounded it near Mersivan (Merzifon) in August 1101 and destroyed it after about five days of fighting. On the way the crusaders sacked a Greek village beyond the Halys. This is the First Crusade's one direct echo in Pontus. The survivors escaped to the coast through Paurai (Bafra) (Bryer 1970, p. 176).", "people": ["Anselm IV of Milan", "Raymond of Toulouse", "Stephen of Blois", "Albert of Biandrate", "Tzitas", "Kilij Arslan I", "Gümüshtigin Gazi"], "sources_primary": ["Albert of Aachen, Historia Ierosolimitana 8.7–23", "Anna Komnene, Alexiad 11.8", "Ekkehard of Aura, Hierosolymita"], "sources_modern": ["Cate 1969/2016, in Setton ed., A History of the Crusades I, pp. 343–356", "Runciman 1952, A History of the Crusades II", "Mulinder 2006", "Bouchard 1998, p. 83", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 176 (the survivors escaped through Paurai)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, p. 162 (the crusaders found the land from Gangra and Kastamon east to Amaseia partly deserted)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 18, 108 (the crusade of 1101 failed to reach Neokaisareia; survivors escaped to the sea from the Merzifon area)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The chronicles give 50,000 or more; modern estimates for the Lombard contingent start at about 10,000 (Bouchard 1998: 'the exaggerated figure of 50,000 … at least 10,000').", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nikomedia", "lat": 40.766, "lon": 29.917, "role": "waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Ankyra", "lat": 39.941, "lon": 32.863, "role": "waypoint 2, taken 23 June 1101"}, {"name": "Gangra", "lat": 40.601, "lon": 33.614, "role": "waypoint 3, failed siege"}, {"name": "Kastamon", "lat": 41.376, "lon": 33.776, "role": "waypoint 4, taken"}, {"name": "Mersivan (Merzifon)", "lat": 40.874, "lon": 35.463, "role": "waypoint 5, defeat, August 1101"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "goal, never reached"}, {"name": "Paurai (Bafra)", "lat": 41.568, "lon": 35.907, "role": "escape route of the survivors to the coast"}]}, {"id": "H3-050", "date": "1103", "date_end": "1106", "date_text": "1103/4 – 1105/6", "precision": "year", "title": "Gregory Taronites rebels at Trapezous", "summary": "Gregory Taronites, a relative of the emperor of Armenian (Taron) descent, had campaigned in Pontus c. 1101–1103. Appointed doux of Chaldia, he rebelled on arrival at Trapezous (1103/4), imprisoning his predecessor Dabatenos and local notables. Alexios sent his cousin John Taronites; Gregory marched inland to Koloneia to seek Danishmendid help from Sebasteia, but John's Frankish mercenaries caught him first. He was imprisoned in the Anemas tower and later pardoned.", "people": ["Gregory Taronites", "John Taronites", "Dabatenos", "Alexios I Komnenos", "Nikephoros Bryennios the Younger"], "sources_primary": ["Anna Komnene, Alexiad 12.7.1–4", "Theophylact of Ohrid, Letters (on his Pontic victories)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970, pp. 175–176", "Skoulatos 1980, Les personnages byzantins de l'Alexiade, pp. 116–118", "ODB 1991, 'Taronites', pp. 2012–2013", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 176 (duke of Chaldia c. 1103, replacing Dabatenos; in Koloneia by 1106, allied with the emir Danishmend; captured by John Komnenos)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 148 (in 1106 Koloneia briefly in the hands of Gregory Taronites, \"alias Gabras ?\", duke of Chaldia, ally of the emir Danishmend)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Whether Gregory Taronites is the same man as Gregory Gabras, Theodore's son, is debated: Hopf, Vasiliev and Cahen say yes, Skoulatos and most others no.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "seat of the rebellion"}, {"name": "Koloneia", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "where Gregory went for help"}, {"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "Danishmendid court"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "prison of Anemas"}]}, {"id": "H3-051", "date": "1119", "date_text": "1119 (Bryer); 1120 or 1121 in other accounts", "precision": "year", "title": "Constantine Gabras allied with the Mengujekids, defeated by Gazi", "summary": "Constantine Gabras, doux of Chaldia, allied himself with the Mengujekid emir of Erzincan, Ibn Mangujak, against the Danishmendid Gümüshtigin Gazi; in 1119 the allies were defeated and men of Gabras's army were taken prisoner (Bryer 1970). The episode shows the duke of Trapezous acting as one more player among the Anatolian emirs.", "people": ["Constantine Gabras", "Gümüshtigin Gazi", "Ishaq (Mengujekid)", "Toghrul Arslan of Melitene"], "sources_primary": ["Michael the Syrian, Chronicle 15 (tr. Chabot III)", "Ibn al-Athir, al-Kamil, s.a. 514 AH"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970", "Cahen 1968, Pre-Ottoman Turkey", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 170 (Ibn Mangujak, the Mengujekid emir of Erzincan, ‘the ally of Constantine Gabras’), p. 181 (‘the prisoners taken from the army of No. 5 [Constantine Gabras] in 1119’)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer (1970, p. 181) dates the defeat and the capture of Gabras's men to 1119, and (p. 170) names Ibn Mangujak as his ally. Whether Gabras himself was captured and ransomed, as later accounts say, is not confirmed in the sources used here.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Erzincan (Keltzene)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.49, "role": "Mengujekid emirate"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Gabras' seat"}]}, {"id": "H3-052", "date": "1126", "date_end": "1140", "date_text": "1126–1140", "precision": "year", "title": "Constantine Gabras, 'tyrant of Trebizond'", "summary": "Constantine Gabras, doux of Chaldia since shortly before 1118, ruled Trapezous practically independently from 1126 and struck his own low-value coinage; Niketas Choniates calls him the 'tyrant of Trebizond'. His exact relation to Theodore is unknown. His rule ended when John II came east with the field army in 1139–40. The metropolitan Stephen Skylitzes, elected about 1126, could not reach his see for fourteen years because of Gabras's rule and arrived only in 1140 (Theodore Prodromos's monody, as used by Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 55-56, 222-230).", "people": ["Constantine Gabras", "John II Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia (tr. Magoulias 1984, p. 69: 'tyrant of Trebizond')", "Coins of Constantine Gabras"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970, p. 171", "Grierson 1982, Byzantine Coins, pp. 228–229", "Angold 1997, The Byzantine Empire 1025–1204, pp. 130, 157", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 55–56; pp. 222–230", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, p. 360 n. 22 (Constantine Gabras brought back the skull of St Theodore Gabras between 1115 and 1140)", "Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic exception', DOP 29 (repr. Bryer 1980, V), p. 117", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 301 (Gabrades ruled Chaldia from about 1075 to after 1140)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether Constantine was Theodore's son (Feldman 2018) or of unknown relation (Bryer) is debated. A 'Song of Gabras' is a modern composition, not a medieval source.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "seat of Constantine Gabras"}]}, {"id": "H3-053", "date": "1132", "date_end": "1135", "date_text": "1132–1135", "precision": "year", "title": "John II takes Kastamon and Gangra from the Danishmendids", "summary": "John II campaigned in Paphlagonia against the Danishmendid Gümüshtigin, taking Kastamon (1132/3), which was lost again, and then Gangra and Kastamon (1134/5). The campaigns reopened the land route along the north of Asia Minor towards Pontus.", "people": ["John II Komnenos", "Gümüshtigin Gazi"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia, ed. van Dieten, pp. 18–21", "John Kinnamos, Epitome 1.5", "Niketas Choniates (Bonn) 25–26, 29", "John Kinnamos (Bonn) 13, 15, 21", "Theodore Prodromos, PG 133, cols. 1373–1383"], "sources_modern": ["Angold 1997", "Magdalino 1993, The Empire of Manuel I Komnenos", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 118–119 (a series of campaigns between 1130 and 1135 recovered Gangra, Kastamon and other towns) and p. 162 n. 176 (Choniates, Bonn, 25–26, 29; Kinnamos, Bonn, 13, 15, 21; Theodore Prodromos, PG 133, 1373–1383; Michael the Syrian III, 234, 249)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Vryonis gives 1130–1135 for the series of campaigns.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kastamon", "lat": 41.376, "lon": 33.776, "role": "taken 1132/3 and 1134/5"}, {"name": "Gangra", "lat": 40.601, "lon": 33.614, "role": "taken 1135"}]}, {"id": "H3-054", "date": "1139", "date_end": "1140", "date_text": "1139–1140", "precision": "year", "title": "John II in Pontus: Gabras submits, Neocaesarea holds out", "summary": "John II marched along the Black Sea coast into Pontus against the Danishmendid emir Muhammad; the show of force ended Constantine Gabras' independence and Chaldia returned to direct rule. In 1140 John besieged Neocaesarea, but lost many horses in the winter and his nephew John went over to the Turks; he withdrew. Neocaesarea and Amaseia stayed Turkish. Around the winter solstice his cavalry grazed at Kinte in the Iris delta, which places the army on the coast. Michael the Syrian says that while John besieged Neocaesarea the Turks killed Christians in their lands who so much as named the emperor (Vryonis 1971, p. 119).", "people": ["John II Komnenos", "Constantine Gabras", "Melik Muhammad (Danishmendid)", "Manuel Komnenos", "John Komnenos (nephew)"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia, ed. van Dieten, pp. 32–35", "John Kinnamos, Epitome 1.6", "Niketas Choniates (Bonn) 45–46 (Kinte)", "Michael the Syrian III, 249"], "sources_modern": ["Angold 1997", "Magdalino 1993", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 55–56; pp. 222–230", "Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic exception', DOP 29 (repr. Bryer 1980, V), p. 128 (John Komnenos grazed his cavalry horses at Kinte, in the Iris delta, at the winter solstice of 1140; Choniates, Bonn, 45–46; Theodore Prodromos, PG 133, 1340–1341)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 118–119 (the expedition against Neocaesarea in 1139–40 failed but marked the high tide of the reconquest; Michael the Syrian on reprisals against Christians in Turkish lands)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 99-100 (John Komnenos' army went into winter quarters at the Pontic polis of Kinte at the winter solstice, 21 December 1139, crossed the mountains in February 1140 and besieged Neocaesarea; Bryer & Winfield tentatively identify Kinte with Idrisi's Kendia and with later Limnia in the Iris delta, rejecting Kündür Köprüsü)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The route along the coast and the year of Gabras' submission (1139 or 1140) are uncertain. Bryer & Winfield (1985) date the wintering at Kinte to the winter of 1139/40 (21 December 1139 to February 1140), a year earlier than Bryer (1975); the siege of Neocaesarea then fell in 1140, and the army was back in Constantinople on 15 January 1141.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "besieged, 1140"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "returned to direct rule"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Ünye)", "lat": 41.128, "lon": 37.288, "role": "coastal approach (probable route)"}]}, {"id": "H3-055", "date": "1157", "date_text": "1157/8", "precision": "year", "title": "A Danishmendid madrasa at Niksar", "summary": "After the Danishmendid lands were divided (1142), the emir Yağıbasan, based at Sebasteia, built a madrasa at Niksar, dated 1157/8 in Turkey's entry for Niksar on the UNESCO tentative list; it is among the oldest Turkish-Islamic buildings in Anatolia. Former Roman cities of Pontus were becoming Muslim urban centres, while keeping Christian populations.", "people": ["Yağıbasan"], "sources_primary": ["Building inscription of the Yağıbasan madrasa"], "sources_modern": ["Cahen 1968", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 108 (\"The earliest dated mosque is of 1180, but there is an earlier Danishmendid medrese\"; no date or inscription given)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 220–221 (the earliest surviving Muslim inscription of Neocaesarea dates from the mid-12th century, after Uzunçarşılı)", "Permanent Delegation of Turkey to UNESCO 2018, tentative list entry 6344, 'Early Period of Anatolian Turkish Heritage: Niksar, the Capital of Danishmend Dynasty' (whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6344): Yağıbasan madrasa 'built in 1157-1158' [Turkish state]"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "Yağıbasan madrasa"}, {"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "Yağıbasan's capital"}]}, {"id": "H3-057", "date": "1173", "date_text": "C. 1173–1180", "precision": "decade", "title": "Andronikos Komnenos raids Chaldia from Koloneia", "summary": "The exiled Andronikos Komnenos, after years at the Georgian court of George III, held a castle near Koloneia, on the border of Chaldia, as a freebooter in the service of the Saltukid emir Mehmed of Erzurum (1173–1176/7), and raided Roman lands from it. Nikephoros Palaiologos, duke of Chaldia at Trapezous, captured his companion Theodora Komnene, the former queen of Jerusalem, and their children; Andronikos then submitted to Manuel I in 1180 (Bryer 1970, p. 180 n. 56). (Continued in C2a-05.)", "people": ["Andronikos Komnenos (later Andronikos I)", "Theodora Komnene", "Nikephoros Palaiologos", "Manuel I Komnenos", "George III of Georgia"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia (Manuel I, on Andronikos' exile)"], "sources_modern": ["Magdalino 1993, The Empire of Manuel I Komnenos", "Kaldellis 2024, The New Roman Empire, p. 697", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 180 n. 56 (held a castle near Koloneia for Mehmed ibn Saltuk of Erzurum, 1173–1176/7; Nikephoros Palaiologos, duke of Chaldia, seized Theodora)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 148 (Mehmet ibn Saltuk granted Andronikos Komnenos a castle near Koloneia in 1173 to 1176/77; Bryer & Winfield ask whether it was Kibossa or Sisorta Kale, on the Saltukid border along the Paryadres)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985, p. 148) suggest, as a question, that Andronikos' castle near Koloneia was Sisorta Kale (33 km WNW of Şebinkarahisar) or Kibossa; no source names it.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Koloneia (castle near it, unlocated)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "area of Andronikos’ castle"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "seat of the doux Nikephoros Palaiologos"}]}, {"id": "H3-056", "date": "1174", "date_end": "1178", "date_text": "1174–1178", "precision": "year", "title": "The Seljuk sultan absorbs the Danishmendids", "summary": "Kilij Arslan II of Rum took over the Danishmendid lands, Sebasteia (1174) and finally Melitene (1178). Two years after his victory over Manuel I at Myriokephalon (1176), one Seljuk power ruled the whole Pontic interior from Amaseia to Sebasteia, while the coast from Sinope to Trapezous stayed Roman. In 1175 Manuel sent Michael Gabras with the troops of Trebizond and Oinaion into the Pontic interior, but he abandoned Amaseia to the sultan and was later arrested for it (Bryer 1970, p. 180).", "people": ["Kilij Arslan II", "Manuel I Komnenos", "Michael Gabras"], "sources_primary": ["Michael the Syrian, Chronicle", "Niketas Choniates, Historia"], "sources_modern": ["Melikoff, 'Dānishmendids', Encyclopaedia of Islam, 2nd ed., vol. 2 (1965), p. 111", "Cahen 1968", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 122–123 (after Nur al-Din’s death in 1174, Sebasteia, Neocaesarea, Dokeia and Komana fell to Kilij Arslan in 1175; Melitene two years later)", "Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), pp. 170 (Neocaesarea taken 1175, the dynasty ended 1178; the Seljuks reached the sea at Amisos soon after), 180 (Michael Gabras sent in 1175 with the troops of Trebizond and Oinaion; he abandoned Amaseia to the sultan)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 101 (Oinaion perhaps in Turkish hands 1157-75, then recovered; it supplied troops to Manuel's expedition of 1175 against the Danishmendids)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The fall of Sebasteia and Neocaesarea is dated 1174 (Melikoff, Cahen) or 1175 (Vryonis 1971; Bryer 1970; Bryer & Winfield 1985, who date the end of the Danishmendids to 1178).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sebasteia", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "taken 1174"}, {"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.425, "lon": 38.362, "role": "taken 1178"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "under Seljuk rule"}, {"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "taken by Kilij Arslan II, 1175"}]}, {"id": "C2a-05", "date": "1180", "date_end": "1182", "date_text": "1180–1182; marched from Oinaion in 1182; emperor 1183–1185", "precision": "year", "title": "Andronikos Komnenos rules the Pontos from Oinaion", "summary": "After submitting to Manuel I in 1180, Andronikos Komnenos was given the Roman Pontos to hold, with Oinaion (Ünye) as his seat; the castle there has been linked with him. In 1182 he set out from Oinaion to seize the throne in Constantinople, and he reigned 1183–1185. His grandsons Alexios and David were the founders of the Empire of Trebizond in 1204, so this brief Pontic lordship is the family’s first tie to the region.", "people": ["Andronikos I Komnenos", "Manuel I Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 180 n. 56 (Andronikos held the Pontos in fief, with Oinaion as his capital, 1180–2; the foundations of his castle)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 126–127 (in 1182 Andronikos set out from Oinaion to claim the throne)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 101-103 (the origins of Oinaion castle may lie in the 12th century, a \"distinct possibility\" that Andronikos lived there with Theodora)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985) are cautious about the castle: its surviving sea wall is comparable to 14th-century imperial work 'but could be earlier', so a 12th-century origin is possible, not proved.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Oinaion (Ünye)", "lat": 41.128, "lon": 37.288, "role": "seat of Andronikos, 1180–1182"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "goal of the march of 1182"}]}, {"id": "C2a-06", "date": "1180", "date_text": "Late 12th c. (Balsamon wrote c. 1170–1195)", "precision": "decade", "title": "The metropolitan of Neocaesarea lives at Oinaion", "summary": "The canonist Theodore Balsamon records that the metropolitan of Neocaesarea, unable to reside in his see because it was in Turkish hands, had been allowed by imperial order and synodal decision to take as his seat his suffragan bishopric of Oinaion, on the coast and still under the emperor. Balsamon adds that many other clergy had fled from towns held by the Turks to bishoprics across the border. The case shows the church of inner Pontus being run from the coast, a century after the Danishmendid conquest.", "people": ["Theodore Balsamon"], "sources_primary": ["Theodore Balsamon, commentary on chapter 20 of Photios’ Nomocanon (as cited by Vryonis 1971, p. 204)"], "sources_modern": ["Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 204–205", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 101 (Oinaion, never itself a see, was the seat of the metropolitan of Neokaisareia from the 12th to the 19th century)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 108 (John II's expedition of 1139/40 moved Greek refugees from the Neokaisareia area into imperial territory \"and probably moved the see to the coast at the same time\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Balsamon's remark can be dated only within his working life (about 1170–1195). Bryer & Winfield (1985) suggest that the see was probably moved to the coast during John II's expedition of 1139/40, so that Balsamon describes an arrangement already some decades old.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Neocaesarea (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "see in Turkish hands"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Ünye)", "lat": 41.128, "lon": 37.288, "role": "residence of the metropolitan"}]}, {"id": "H3-058", "date": "1184", "date_end": "1202", "date_text": "1184 (Tamar's sole rule) – 1202 (Basiani)", "precision": "year", "title": "Tamar's Georgia: the new power on Chaldia's eastern border", "summary": "Under Queen Tamar (sole ruler from 1184), Georgia became the strongest Christian state of the region; her army defeated the Seljuk sultan Rukn al-Din at Basiani (1202). Her court sheltered Alexios and David Komnenos, grandsons of Andronikos I through his son Manuel and the Georgian princess Rusudan. Georgia, not Constantinople, would be the base for the Komnenoi's seizure of Trapezous in 1204.", "people": ["Tamar of Georgia", "Rukn al-Din Süleymanshah II", "Alexios Komnenos", "David Komnenos", "Rusudan"], "sources_primary": ["Histories and Eulogies of the Sovereigns (Kartlis Tskhovreba)", "Basili Ezosmodzghvari, Life of Tamar"], "sources_modern": ["Rapp 2003, Studies in Medieval Georgian Historiography", "Vasiliev 1936, 'The foundation of the Empire of Trebizond', Speculum 11, pp. 3–37", "Eastmond ed. 2016, Byzantium’s Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48 (Basiani dated 1203)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The exact kinship of Tamar and the Komnenoi is debated (Panaretos calls her their relative; see chapter 6). Eastmond (2016) dates Basiani to 1203.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Basiani (Pasinler plain)", "lat": 39.98, "lon": 41.68, "role": "Georgian victory, 1202 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Tiflis", "lat": 41.693, "lon": 44.801, "role": "Tamar's capital"}]}, {"id": "C2a-04", "date": "1188", "date_text": "1188 (colophon)", "precision": "year", "title": "A Jewish community at Trebizond", "summary": "The colophon of a Bible scroll records its purchase in 1188 from ‘the community of our brethren the kahal of Tirapzin’, the first record of Jews at Trebizond; it is published in Ankori’s study of the Karaites. The city already drew Muslim, Armenian, Georgian and northern merchants; Jewish traders are now attested among them.", "sources_primary": ["Colophon of a Bible scroll, 1188 (published by Ankori 1959, pp. 122–125)"], "sources_modern": ["Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), p. 211", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, p. 16 n. 86 (‘the earliest mention of Jews in Byzantine Trebizond is 1188’)", "Ankori 1959, Karaites in Byzantium, pp. 122–125 (via Sheard and Vryonis)"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Jewish community (kahal)"}]}, {"id": "C2a-07", "date": "1190", "date_end": "1200", "date_text": "After 1178 and by the 1190s (Amisos Turkish); 1200 (Frangopoulos)", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Seljuks reach the Black Sea at Amisos", "summary": "After absorbing the Danishmendids, the Seljuks of Konya pushed down the Halys and Iris to the coast and took Amisos (Samsun) before the end of the 12th century, their first port on the Black Sea. Sinope and the coast east of the Iris stayed Roman. In 1200 Alexios III sent Constantine Frangopoulos into the Black Sea, nominally to look into a ship wrecked near Kerasous, in fact to plunder the merchantmen sailing to and from Amisos; among his victims were merchants from Ikonion (Konya), and the sultan obtained 50 minae of silver in compensation, on top of the 300 minae a year that the emperor already paid him.", "people": ["Kilij Arslan II", "Rukn al-Din Suleymanshah II", "Alexios III Angelos", "Constantine Frangopoulos"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia (ed. van Dieten) 528–529 (Frangopoulos); Bonn 689 (Amisos)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1970, 'A Byzantine family: the Gabrades' (repr. Bryer 1980, IIIa), p. 170 (‘they reached the sea at Amisos soon after’ 1178)", "Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, p. 161 (by the end of the 12th c. the Turks held Amisos)", "Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic exception', DOP 29 (repr. Bryer 1980, V), p. 126 (the Seljuks’ efforts to reach the coast down the Halys at the turn of the 12th c.)", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), pp. 213–215 (Frangopoulos and the Ikonion merchants)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Sheard suspects that Choniates wrote 'Phasis' for another port in describing the wrecked ship's route.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amisos (Samsun)", "lat": 41.302, "lon": 36.335, "role": "Seljuk port by the 1190s"}, {"name": "Kerasous", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.389, "role": "shipwreck used as pretext, 1200"}, {"name": "Ikonion (Konya)", "lat": 37.875, "lon": 32.493, "role": "home of the plundered merchants"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "still Roman (lost 1214)"}]}], "turkish": [], "synthesis": "## A coast ruled from the far end of its own sea, 330–520\n\nWhen Constantine dedicated Constantinople in 330, the Pontic coast found its capital at the western end of a sea lane that ran by Sinope and Amisos to Trapezous. For the next nine centuries the Greeks of Pontus were tied to the emperors mainly by ship. Inland, the old kingdom had been cut into small provinces: Helenopontus, with its metropolis at Amaseia; Pontus Polemoniacus, with its metropolis at Neocaesarea, to which Trapezous and Kerasous belonged; and, from about 386, Armenia I and II, made out of Lesser Armenia. The partition of Armenia with Persia, about 387, and the building of Theodosiopolis, later Erzurum, put the frontier on the upper Euphrates, and Pontus became the rear of an army. John Chrysostom died at Komana in 407 on his way to exile at Pityous, which shows the roads that joined the capital, the Pontic interior and the Caucasian coast.\n\nThe bishops of Trapezous appear in the records of the councils. At Chalcedon in 451 Atarbios, \"bishop of the city of Trapezous\", signed for himself and for his metropolitan, Dorotheos of Neocaesarea; in 518 Antipatros of Trapezous signed against Severus of Antioch at a synod in Constantinople; and in 535 Anthimos of Trapezous became patriarch of Constantinople, only to be deposed within the year under pressure from Pope Agapetus, leaving his own church, in the words of the record, \"widowed\".\n\n## Justinian and the Lazic War, 522–562\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch05_lazica.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch05_lazica.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1440\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the eastern Black Sea from Trapezous to Lazica and Iberia, showing Petra, Archaeopolis, Phasis and Telephis, and the frontier towns given new defences.\"></a><figcaption>Lazica and the frontier in Justinian's time: the sieges and battles of the Lazic War, and the towns of the frontier given new defences. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nIn 522 the Lazic king Tzath I was baptised in Constantinople, and the eastern Black Sea coast changed sides from Persia to Rome. Justinian then took the Pontic hinterland in hand. His general Sittas subdued and baptised the Tzanoi, the highland raiders behind Trapezous, and roads and forts were built among them; a field command for Armenia was created; and two laws of 535–536 (Novels 28 and 31) redrew the provinces. Pontus Polemoniacus disappeared: Neocaesarea and Polemonion went to Helenopontus, and Trapezous and Kerasous to a new Armenia I governed from Justinianopolis, whose site is uncertain. Trapezous was a city of that province, not its capital. Procopius records an aqueduct at Trapezous named after its martyr Eugenios, restored churches there and at Amaseia, and new defences at Rhizaion, Satala, Koloneia and Theodosiopolis (*Buildings* 3). The walls of Trapezous are often credited to Justinian, but Procopius does not mention them, and the one inscription cited for them records a renewal of the city's buildings, not its walls (Bryer & Winfield 1985; Eastmond 2016).\n\nThe Lazic War was fought just east of Pontus. It had a prelude in the 520s: when the Persian king Kavad attacked Iberia, its king Gourgenes fled into Lazica \"with all the notables of the Iberians\", and the Romans gave up two Lazic forts, which the Persians occupied (Procopius, *Wars* 1.12). In 541 the Lazi, angered by the trade monopoly that the Roman general John Tzibus held at Petra, invited Chosroes I in. Procopius believed that the Persian king wanted a base on the Black Sea from which his ships could reach Constantinople. The Romans besieged Petra in 549 with 7,000 men and 1,000 Tzanoi (Procopius, *Wars* 2.29), and stormed and razed it in 551. Archaeopolis held, but the Romans were beaten at Telephis in 554; in 555 Roman officers murdered the Lazic king Gubazes; and in 556 the Persians failed before Phasis. The Tzanoi rose again in 557. The Fifty Years' Peace of 561/2 left Lazica Roman, at the price of 30,000 gold coins a year to Persia (Menander, fragment 6.1). Bessarion, writing in the 1430s, says that Trapezous fought beside the Roman armies against the Persians, showed them the roads and passes and served as their base; we have found no earlier source that says so.\n\n## Persians, Arabs and the themes, 602–950\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch05_themes.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch05_themes.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Pontus and Armenia Minor showing the Arab raids and battles of 640–863, the Paulician state of Tephrike, the themes of Armeniakon, Koloneia and Chaldia, and the sees under Trapezous about 900.\"></a><figcaption>Persians, Arabs and the themes, 602–950: raids, battles and sacks named in this chapter, the Paulicians of Tephrike, the themes, and the sees under the metropolitan of Trapezous about 900. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nThe great Persian war of 602–628 reached the edge of Pontus: Theodosiopolis fell, Caesarea was occupied and Euchaita was burned. Herakleios left his army to winter \"in Pontus\" in 622/3; after the battle on the Saros in 625 Theophanes has him winter at Sebasteia; and in 626–627 he worked from Lazica with the Turks before Tiflis, before striking south to Nineveh. It is often said that he campaigned \"via Trapezous\", or wintered there, but no ancient source names the city. Bryer and Winfield (1978) argue that his base for Lazica in 626 was the old Roman camp at Sourmaina, renamed Herakleia (today's Araklı, east of Trapezous); other historians name Trapezous itself as his port. In the same decades the Armenian scholar Anania of Shirak, after searching in vain for a teacher at home, studied mathematics for eight years at Trapezous with the teacher Tychikos, whom he found at the shrine of St Eugenios.\n\nThe Arab raids began in 640 at Euchaita. Maslama took Amaseia in 712; in 811 Arab raiders seized the pay of the Armeniakon army, 1,300 pounds of gold, near Euchaita; the emperor Theophilos was beaten at Anzen near Dazimon in 838; and in 863 Umar, emir of Melitene, sacked Amisos before Petronas destroyed his army at Lalakaon. No raid is recorded across the Pontic Alps to Trapezous: the mountains protected the coast. In the same centuries the Paulicians, a dualist sect, began at Kibossa near Koloneia, and later built a fortified state at Tephrike, which was destroyed in 872–878. In 838 the Khurramite Persians, refugees from the caliphate serving in the Roman army, proclaimed their commander Theophobos emperor at Sinope.\n\nThe armies settled in the provinces became the \"themes\". The Armeniakon had its headquarters at Amaseia and is first clearly recorded in 667/8. Chaldia was split off from it, with Trapezous as its capital; it was a theme by 842/3, and perhaps from about 819–824. Koloneia was split off about 842 and was a theme by 863. Arab geographers counted the forts and men of these themes, and Constantine VII recorded that the strategos of Chaldia was paid 20 pounds of gold, half of it from the customs of Trapezous. The governors of Trapezous learned early to act on their own: in 923 the strategos Bardas Boilas rebelled with local magnates and seized Bayburt, and John Kourkouas put him down (Bryer & Winfield 1985). After the reconquest of Melitene in 934 and Theodosiopolis in 949, Pontus lay well behind the frontier for a century.\n\nThe church followed the cities. Theodoros of Trapezous signed the acts of the Sixth Ecumenical Council in 680, and at the second Council of Nicaea in 787 Christophoros signed as \"bishop of Phasis, that is of Trapezous\". Chrysanthos argued from this that Trapezous had already taken over the metropolitan rank of Phasis; others date its rise to the time of Patriarch Methodios, about 843–847. By the early 10th century the metropolis of Trapezous had seven suffragan sees in the Notitia of Leo VI, Cheriana, Chamatzour, Chaliou, Paiper (Bayburt), Kerameos, Leriou and Bizanon, and later lists add more, reaching inland towards Kelkit, Tercan and Pasin. Most of these sees cannot be located with any certainty. An inscription in the Chrysokephalos, the metropolitan church, records that its throne was renewed under metropolitan Basil in 913/914. The small church of St Anne, restored in 884/5, is the only church built before 1204 that still stands in the city (Eastmond 2004).\n\n## Trapezous the market\n\nThe Muslim geographers of the 10th century knew Trebizond as the Roman frontier market. Al-Mas'udi writes of a fair visited by Muslim, Roman and Armenian merchants and traders from the Caucasus, held \"once a year\" in Pellat's translation (as quoted by Sheard 2021, p. 203) or \"several\" times a year in Vryonis' reading of another edition (1971, p. 15). Al-Istakhri and Ibn Hawqal say that the Roman silks sold in Islamic lands came through Trebizond. John Lazaropoulos, a 14th-century metropolitan, ties the great fair to the feast of St Eugenios' birthday on 24 June, which he says was first kept under Basil I. Bryer & Winfield, Vryonis and Sheard accept the link; Rosenqvist (1996) thinks the story as a whole suspect. The city also fed the capital: in the revolt of Bardas Phokas, about 988, Basil II ordered the coastal towns \"as far as Trebizond\" to ship grain to Constantinople. A Bible scroll bought in 1188 from \"the community of our brethren the kahal of Tirapzin\" is the first record of Jews at Trebizond (Ankori). In 1021/2 the city even housed the emperor: Basil II wintered there during his war with George I of Georgia, and there, in December 1021, Senekerim of Vaspurakan surrendered his kingdom.\n\nJohn Xiphilinos, born at Trebizond about 1010, wrote as a young man a collection of miracles of St Eugenios that records events in the city in his own and his family's lifetime. He rose to be patriarch of Constantinople (1064–1075), as Anthimos of Trapezous had been five centuries before.\n\n## Manzikert and the Turks, 1059–1180\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch05_turks.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch05_turks.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Pontus from Sinope to Manzikert showing the towns held for the emperors after 1071, the Danishmendid towns, the battle of Mersivan in 1101 and the taking of Trapezous from Georgia in 1204.\"></a><figcaption>Manzikert and the Turks: the towns still held for the emperors, the Danishmendid towns of about 1075–1085, the battles named in this chapter, and the expedition from Georgia that took Trapezous in April 1204. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nTurkmen raids sacked Sebasteia in 1059, Caesarea in 1067 and Neocaesarea in 1068. After Manzikert in 1071 the eastern armies dissolved in civil war, and the Norman mercenary Roussel de Bailleul ruled round Amaseia until the young Alexios Komnenos bought him from a Turkish emir. Within about fifteen years the Danishmendid emir held Amaseia, Neocaesarea, Tokat, Komana and Sebasteia. The Turks' own memory of these conquests is the *Danishmendname*, an epic written down in the 14th century, which tells the deeds of Melik Danishmend Gazi at Sebasteia, Tokat, Niksar and Amasya as a holy war and joins his story to that of the 8th-century hero Battal Gazi. The dates come from Michael the Syrian, who has Danishmend ruling \"Cappadocia\" in 1085, and from Anna Komnene; Cahen (1968) and Beihammer (2017) place the conquests between about 1075 and 1085, and the Christian townspeople stayed under Danishmendid rule. A Turkish emir, Karatekin, even took Sinope, about 1081, and it was recovered for Alexios only after the death of the sultan Sulayman in 1086 (Anna Komnene).\n\nThe coast round Trapezous was held by a local man. Theodore Gabras recovered Trapezous from the Turks about 1075 and held it, in Anna Komnene's phrase, \"as a prize that had fallen to his own lot\". Alexios I, after he came to the throne in 1081, recognised him as doux of Chaldia, but kept his son Gregory in Constantinople as a hostage. Theodore was killed by the Turks in 1098 or 1099 and is venerated as a martyr. His successors acted much as he had: Gregory Taronites rebelled at Trapezous in 1103/4 and was caught on his way to seek Danishmendid help; Constantine Gabras allied himself with the Mengujekid emir of Erzincan against the Danishmendids and was defeated in 1119; and he ruled as a near-independent \"tyrant of Trebizond\" from 1126 until John II's Pontic campaign of 1139–40, which restored direct rule but failed before Neocaesarea. Historians disagree whether this was autonomy or loyal service at a distance: Bryer (1970) speaks of a personal fief, while Feldman (2018) reads the family's seals as those of men secure in imperial titles.\n\nThe First Crusade touched Pontus once. In August 1100 the Danishmendid emir captured Bohemond, prince of Antioch, and held him at Neocaesarea. In 1101 a large Lombard-led crusade marched across the Halys to free him and was surrounded and destroyed near Mersivan (Merzifon) after about five days of fighting; the survivors escaped to the coast through Bafra. Alexios I offered 260,000 dinars to have Bohemond handed over to him; Bohemond was ransomed instead in 1103, with help from the Armenian lord Kogh Vasil, for 100,000 to 130,000 dinars, as modern historians give the sum from Matthew of Edessa (MacEvitt 2010; Morton 2020).\n\nThe Seljuk sultan Kilij Arslan II absorbed the Danishmendids in 1174–78, and before the end of the century the Seljuks reached the sea at Amisos, their first Black Sea port. By 1180 the line was clear: a Turkish interior from Amaseia to Sebasteia, and a Roman coast from Sinope to Trapezous. The Christians of the interior stayed under Turkish rule, but their church was run from the coast: the canonist Balsamon records that the metropolitan of Neocaesarea, unable to live in his see, had been allowed to take as his seat the coastal bishopric of Oinaion (Ünye). Historians disagree how fast the Greek Christianity of the interior declined: Vryonis (1971) sees the flight of the clergy as the start of a long decline, while others stress how piecemeal the Turkish settlement was and how long Christian townspeople remained.\n\n## The road to 1204\n\nThe family that would found the Empire of Trebizond first came to Pontus in these years. The exiled Andronikos Komnenos raided Chaldia from a castle near Koloneia, which he held for the Saltukid emir of Erzurum, about 1173–1180; after he submitted to Manuel I in 1180 he was given the Pontus to hold, with Oinaion as his seat, and from there in 1182 he set out to seize the throne. His son Manuel married a Georgian princess, Rusudan, and their sons Alexios and David grew up at the court of Queen Tamar, under whom Georgia became the strongest Christian state of the region. In April 1204 the brothers took Trapezous with Georgian help. Panaretos, the chronicler of Trebizond, calls Tamar their kinswoman; how much help she gave, and why, is disputed. It was the same month in which the crusaders sacked Constantinople, and which came first is not known.\n\n## The numbers, and who gives them\n\n| What | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Roman force before Petra, 549 | 7,000 Romans and 1,000 Tzanoi | Procopius, *Wars* 2.29 |\n| Payment to Persia under the peace of 561/2 | 30,000 gold coins a year (about 400 pounds of gold) | Menander, fragment 6.1 |\n| The Armeniakon in the 9th century | about 9,000 men and 17 forts; the strategos paid 40 pounds of gold | the Arab lists and Leo VI's salary list (*Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium*) |\n| Chaldia | 6 forts; 4,000 men, or 10,000 | Ibn Khurradadhbih; Qudama; Ibn al-Faqih (generally thought too high) |\n| Armenian emigrants who founded Hamamashen, about 790 | about 12,000 | Ghewond, a chronicler's round number |\n| Pay of the Armeniakon seized by the Arabs, 811 | 1,300 pounds of gold | Theophanes |\n| Umar's army, 863 | 8,000; 40,000 | al-Ya'qubi; Genesios and Theophanes Continuatus; Haldon estimates 15,000–20,000 |\n| Senekerim's following, 1021/2 | 14,000 | Garsoïan (1997); Holmes (2005) |\n| The crusade of 1101 | 50,000 or more | the chronicles; modern estimates for the Lombards start at about 10,000 |\n| Bohemond's ransom, 1103 | 100,000–130,000 dinars | Matthew of Edessa, as given by MacEvitt and Morton |\n\n## Legend and evidence\n\nSoumela, the most famous monastery of Pontus, is said to have been founded in 386 by two monks of Athens, Barnabas and Sophronios, who brought an icon painted by St Luke to a cave on Mount Mela. No text, inscription or building of the 4th to 12th centuries supports the story; its earliest written forms are of the 18th century, and the monastery's documented history begins with the Grand Komnenoi, above all with the chrysobull of Alexios III of December 1364. The traditional founding dates of Vazelon (270 or 317) and of Peristera (752, when three monks were led to the site by doves) are likewise tradition; Vazelon's real record is its own acts of the 13th to 15th centuries. Umar flogging the sea at Amisos in 863 is a literary borrowing from the story of Xerxes, and the \"Song of Gabras\" is a modern composition.\n\n## The Greeks of Pontus and their neighbours\n\nIn these centuries the Greeks of Pontus became the \"Romans\" of the Black Sea frontier: Greek-speaking and Orthodox, gathered round the coastal cities, the inland bishoprics of Amaseia, Neocaesarea, Komana and Koloneia, and the shrines of their saints. Trapezous drew Armenian scholars and Muslim merchants, and by the 11th century its leading families could hold the city alone. After 1071 the inland Greeks of Amaseia, Neocaesarea and Tokat passed under Muslim rule, while the coast stayed with Constantinople; the split of 1204, a Christian coast and a Turkish interior, was in place by 1180. Medieval Chaldia, the coast and hinterland of Trapezous, is not the same as the later metropolis of Chaldia round Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane).\n\nTheir neighbours were many. The Persians fought Rome for Lazica and invaded in the early 7th century. The Laz were Roman clients after 522, by turns Rome's allies and its victims, and the coast east of Rhizaion stayed Laz in speech. The Tzanoi, highlanders between Trapezous and Armenia, were baptised under Justinian and later absorbed into Chaldia. Armenians lived in Lesser Armenia from the start; emigrants founded Hamamashen (Hemşin) above Rhizaion about 790; Armenian soldiers and nobles served in the themes; and between 1021 and 1065 the royal houses of Vaspurakan, Ani and Kars were resettled with their followings round Sebasteia and in Cappadocia, Gagik-Abas of Kars receiving Amaseia and Komana among other towns (Vryonis 1971; Bryer 1970). Armenian chroniclers blame Constantinople for stripping the frontier of its defenders; Greek writers of the 1070s blame Armenian disloyalty. To the east lay the Georgians: David of Tao, whose lands Basil II annexed in 1001, and Queen Tamar's kingdom. The Arabs raided the inland towns for three centuries, never Trapezous as far as the record shows, and were also its customers. From about 1075 the interior belonged to Turkish dynasties: the Danishmendids at Amaseia, Neocaesarea and Sebasteia, the Mengujekids at Erzincan and Koloneia, the Saltukids at Erzurum, and, from 1174–78, the Seljuks of Rum. A Danishmendid madrasa at Niksar, dated 1157/8 in Turkey's entry on the UNESCO tentative list, is among the oldest Turkish-Islamic buildings in Anatolia.\n\n## The sources\n\nProcopius is the main narrative for Justinian's age and the Lazic War, and his *Buildings* lists the forts, churches and works of the reign. Agathias continues the Lazic War to 557, and Menander gives the treaty of 561/2 in detail. Justinian's Novels set out the provinces. Theophanes is the main chronicle for 602–813; Theophanes Continuatus and Genesios cover the 9th century, and Constantine VII describes the themes, their pay and their customs. The Arab geographers, Ibn Khurradadhbih, Qudama, al-Mas'udi, al-Istakhri and Ibn Hawqal, describe the themes and the trade of Trebizond from outside, with figures for forts and men. Anna Komnene is the main source for the Gabras family, Taronites and Roussel; Niketas Choniates and John Kinnamos cover John II, Constantine Gabras and Andronikos. Matthew of Edessa and Aristakes give the Armenian accounts of the 11th century; Michael the Syrian gives the Danishmendids; Ghewond the emigration of about 790; Peter of Sicily the Paulicians; Albert of Aachen the crusade of 1101; and Panaretos April 1204. The *Danishmendname* is the Turkish epic of the conquest of the interior. The church records come from the acts of the councils and the lists of sees, as gathered by Chrysanthos (1933), and from the hagiography of St Eugenios edited by Rosenqvist (1996).\n\nAmong modern works, Bryer & Winfield's *Byzantine Monuments and Topography of the Pontos* (1985) is the standard topography; Bryer (1970) and Dunn, Bryer & Nesbitt (2003) treat the Gabras family; Greatrex & Lieu (2002) and Braund (1994) the 6th-century frontier and Lazica; Kaegi (2003) and Howard-Johnston (1999) Herakleios, disagreeing on his routes; the *Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium* (1991) is the general reference; Pertusi (1952) and Oikonomides (1972) edit the texts behind the dates of the themes; Vryonis (1971), Cahen (1968) and Beihammer (2017) treat the Turkish conquest, Vryonis stressing destruction, Cahen continuity; Yazıcı's article \"Danishmendids\" in the *Encyclopaedia Iranica* (1993) gives the dynasty; Garsoïan (1967) and Lemerle (1973) give the two classic views of the Paulicians; Dédéyan (2003) and Garsoïan (1998) the Armenian resettlements; Haldon (1999, 2001) and Treadgold (1995, 1997) the armies; Eastmond (2004, 2016) the art and city of Trebizond; and Sheard (2021) its trade.\n"};
